{"parentUuid":"e20c9aee-e3ae-4599-b165-51a2f6b0d260","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvSyr71Qd44SVRW4omS9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The worktree is clean and current. I'm making the edits: the facts line and lede, the nav, the workflows and evals captions, the two new sections, and Get started."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":324,"cache_read_input_tokens":840513,"output_tokens":2090,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":26},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":324,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2090,"cache_read_input_tokens":840513,"cache_creation_input_tokens":324,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":324},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"474707f9-a787-4299-aee6-676619176742","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgvSyqkRyP1CWLEEVBUcY","type":"assistant","uuid":"97b91561-733b-4c0b-a3e8-9ea6aa56fcbc","timestamp":"2026-11-11T03:42:08.642Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
{"parentUuid":"97b91561-733b-4c0b-a3e8-9ea6aa56fcbc","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvSyr71Qd44SVRW4omS9","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FHeGdbJQoi7mmgbpdgbvZd","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion-gh-pages && python3 - <<'EOF'\np='index.html'\ns=open(p).read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(a)==1,a[:80]; s=s.replace(a,b)\nrep(\"\"\"It's early, and the full pipeline is still in progress.</p>\n  <p class=\"facts\">Rust · Tauri 2 + Leptos · on <a href=\"https://iii.dev\">iii</a> 0.25.0 · macOS and Linux</p>\"\"\",\n\"\"\"It's early: this is 0.1.0, and the Mac app isn't signed with a Developer ID yet.</p>\n  <p class=\"facts\">Rust · iced · on <a href=\"https://iii.dev\">iii</a> 0.25.0 · Apple Silicon Macs and Linux · v0.1.0</p>\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"    <a href=\"#use-cases\">use cases</a>\"\"\",\"\"\"    <a href=\"#use-cases\">use cases</a>\n    <a href=\"#watch\">tray</a>\n    <a href=\"#after\">after the merge</a>\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"An illustration. Bastion ships three workflows (short, full, and full without acceptance tests). Copy one to make your own, and each job keeps the version it started on. An admin edits workflows, and everyone else picks one per request. The workflow model and its API are built; the canvas for editing them is in progress.\"\"\",\n\"\"\"An illustration. Bastion ships three built-in workflows (short, full, and full without acceptance tests) and a dozen more to start from. An admin changes any of them on the canvas, or starts a new one from a name. A changed built-in takes the original's place, and reset brings the original back. Each job keeps the version it started on.\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"Today an arm changes rule rungs. Changing the model per step or the workflow is planned.</p>\"\"\",\n\"\"\"Today an arm changes rule rungs. Each trial also runs its contract, the workspace's gates and the repository's <code>verify.yaml</code>, so a grade sits beside a check that passed or didn't.</p>\"\"\")\ni=s.index(\"\"\"<section>\n  <h2>What it is not</h2>\"\"\")\nnew='''<section id=\"watch\">\n  <h2>Watch it with the window closed</h2>\n  <p class=\"title\">Close the window. The server and the tray keep going.</p>\n  <p>The server outlives the window, and so does the tray icon beside it. The tray says how many jobs wait on you and how many run. It lists the waiting jobs so you can approve one, and it pauses or opens intake. A job that comes to wait, fails, or lands shows up as a system notification, and it can go to a chat webhook too.</p>\n  <div class=\"note\"><b>j33b65192 waits for you at plan</b> · Add twice(x) to calc.py<br><span>the tray reads 2 waiting, the same count the server gives</span></div>\n  <p class=\"caption\">From a recorded run. On macOS the count sits beside a white castle in the menu bar.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"after\">\n  <h2>After the merge</h2>\n  <p class=\"title\">A landed change still answers for itself.</p>\n  <p>The pull request carries the plan, the briefing, a UAT script, the proof, and anything you waived. Only the person who asked for the change can sign off its UAT. After the merge, Bastion watches the merge commit's deployment. If a later commit reverts the change, that revert counts as an escape against the job that landed it.</p>\n  <p>The learn step then proposes rule changes, each with evidence from the job. Accept one and it becomes a constraint or a workspace gate. The Verification page lists each landed job with the decisions that let it through and the escapes found since.</p>\n  <div class=\"note\"><b>learn</b> · the autofix deleted the requested <code># TODO: tidy</code> line and the test assertion that checked for it, and pytest still passed<br><span>it proposed two constraints and a gate, each quoting the job</span></div>\n  <p class=\"caption\">From recorded runs: a learn turn on a local repository, and a pull request on a demo repository that merged, deployed nothing, and was reverted.</p>\n</section>\n\n'''\ns=s[:i]+new+s[i:]\ni=s.index('<section id=\"start\">'); j=s.index('</section>',i)+len('</section>')\ns=s[:i]+'''<section id=\"start\">\n  <h2>Get started</h2>\n  <p>Install the server and the CLI, or add <code>--desktop</code> for the app and the tray too. The installer checks each download against the release's signed checksums.</p>\n<pre>curl -fsSL https://tacoda.github.io/bastion/install.sh | sh\ncurl -fsSL https://tacoda.github.io/bastion/install.sh | sh -s -- --desktop</pre>\n  <p>Or use Homebrew. The app isn't signed with a Developer ID yet, so the cask needs <code>--no-quarantine</code>.</p>\n<pre>brew install tacoda/bastion/bastion\nbrew install --cask --no-quarantine tacoda/bastion/bastion</pre>\n  <p>On a Linux VM or VPS, <code>--service</code> runs the server as a systemd user service. The host needs <code>/dev/kvm</code> for the sandbox VMs.</p>\n  <p>You also need <code>iii</code> 0.25.0 and a key for one model provider, which you add in Settings. Start the app or <code>bastion-server</code>. If something is missing, Doctor names it and says how to fix it. Later, <code>bastion update</code> installs a newer release once its signature checks.</p>\n</section>'''+s[j:]\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ncp /Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/scripts/install.sh install.sh; git status --short","description":"Update the site and add install.sh"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":324,"cache_read_input_tokens":840513,"output_tokens":2090,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":26},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":324,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2090,"cache_read_input_tokens":840513,"cache_creation_input_tokens":324,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":324},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01FHeGdbJQoi7mmgbpdgbvZd":{"command":"cd /Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion-gh-pages && python3 - <<'EOF'\np='index.html'\ns=open(p).read()\ndef rep(a,b):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(a)==1,a[:80]; s=s.replace(a,b)\nrep(\"\"\"It's early, and the full pipeline is still in progress.</p>\n  <p class=\"facts\">Rust · Tauri 2 + Leptos · on <a href=\"https://iii.dev\">iii</a> 0.25.0 · macOS and Linux</p>\"\"\",\n\"\"\"It's early: this is 0.1.0, and the Mac app isn't signed with a Developer ID yet.</p>\n  <p class=\"facts\">Rust · iced · on <a href=\"https://iii.dev\">iii</a> 0.25.0 · Apple Silicon Macs and Linux · v0.1.0</p>\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"    <a href=\"#use-cases\">use cases</a>\"\"\",\"\"\"    <a href=\"#use-cases\">use cases</a>\n    <a href=\"#watch\">tray</a>\n    <a href=\"#after\">after the merge</a>\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"An illustration. Bastion ships three workflows (short, full, and full without acceptance tests). Copy one to make your own, and each job keeps the version it started on. An admin edits workflows, and everyone else picks one per request. The workflow model and its API are built; the canvas for editing them is in progress.\"\"\",\n\"\"\"An illustration. Bastion ships three built-in workflows (short, full, and full without acceptance tests) and a dozen more to start from. An admin changes any of them on the canvas, or starts a new one from a name. A changed built-in takes the original's place, and reset brings the original back. Each job keeps the version it started on.\"\"\")\nrep(\"\"\"Today an arm changes rule rungs. Changing the model per step or the workflow is planned.</p>\"\"\",\n\"\"\"Today an arm changes rule rungs. Each trial also runs its contract, the workspace's gates and the repository's <code>verify.yaml</code>, so a grade sits beside a check that passed or didn't.</p>\"\"\")\ni=s.index(\"\"\"<section>\n  <h2>What it is not</h2>\"\"\")\nnew='''<section id=\"watch\">\n  <h2>Watch it with the window closed</h2>\n  <p class=\"title\">Close the window. The server and the tray keep going.</p>\n  <p>The server outlives the window, and so does the tray icon beside it. The tray says how many jobs wait on you and how many run. It lists the waiting jobs so you can approve one, and it pauses or opens intake. A job that comes to wait, fails, or lands shows up as a system notification, and it can go to a chat webhook too.</p>\n  <div class=\"note\"><b>j33b65192 waits for you at plan</b> · Add twice(x) to calc.py<br><span>the tray reads 2 waiting, the same count the server gives</span></div>\n  <p class=\"caption\">From a recorded run. On macOS the count sits beside a white castle in the menu bar.</p>\n</section>\n\n<section id=\"after\">\n  <h2>After the merge</h2>\n  <p class=\"title\">A landed change still answers for itself.</p>\n  <p>The pull request carries the plan, the briefing, a UAT script, the proof, and anything you waived. Only the person who asked for the change can sign off its UAT. After the merge, Bastion watches the merge commit's deployment. If a later commit reverts the change, that revert counts as an escape against the job that landed it.</p>\n  <p>The learn step then proposes rule changes, each with evidence from the job. Accept one and it becomes a constraint or a workspace gate. The Verification page lists each landed job with the decisions that let it through and the escapes found since.</p>\n  <div class=\"note\"><b>learn</b> · the autofix deleted the requested <code># TODO: tidy</code> line and the test assertion that checked for it, and pytest still passed<br><span>it proposed two constraints and a gate, each quoting the job</span></div>\n  <p class=\"caption\">From recorded runs: a learn turn on a local repository, and a pull request on a demo repository that merged, deployed nothing, and was reverted.</p>\n</section>\n\n'''\ns=s[:i]+new+s[i:]\ni=s.index('<section id=\"start\">'); j=s.index('</section>',i)+len('</section>')\ns=s[:i]+'''<section id=\"start\">\n  <h2>Get started</h2>\n  <p>Install the server and the CLI, or add <code>--desktop</code> for the app and the tray too. The installer checks each download against the release's signed checksums.</p>\n<pre>curl -fsSL https://tacoda.github.io/bastion/install.sh | sh\ncurl -fsSL https://tacoda.github.io/bastion/install.sh | sh -s -- --desktop</pre>\n  <p>Or use Homebrew. The app isn't signed with a Developer ID yet, so the cask needs <code>--no-quarantine</code>.</p>\n<pre>brew install tacoda/bastion/bastion\nbrew install --cask --no-quarantine tacoda/bastion/bastion</pre>\n  <p>On a Linux VM or VPS, <code>--service</code> runs the server as a systemd user service. The host needs <code>/dev/kvm</code> for the sandbox VMs.</p>\n  <p>You also need <code>iii</code> 0.25.0 and a key for one model provider, which you add in Settings. Start the app or <code>bastion-server</code>. If something is missing, Doctor names it and says how to fix it. Later, <code>bastion update</code> installs a newer release once its signature checks.</p>\n</section>'''+s[j:]\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nEOF\ncp /Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/scripts/install.sh install.sh; git status --short","description":"Update the site and add install.sh"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"474707f9-a787-4299-aee6-676619176742","apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CgvSyqkRyP1CWLEEVBUcY","type":"assistant","uuid":"0bf30145-2337-4180-bdc8-b881cf9a6344","timestamp":"2026-11-11T03:42:24.160Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranc