{"parentUuid":"43c9bff0-5654-449b-b7ff-1f9e09bc1808","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"7c304424-caf5-48fd-bfdc-5e1da322b9a0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_012k8JvaoAiys3LG9bvyESh1","content":"Launching skill: voice-check"}]},"uuid":"bfa5b249-2036-4423-8842-6561fe913a19","timestamp":"2026-11-11T03:42:46.670Z","toolUseResult":{"success":true,"commandName":"voice-check"},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"4c9a921d-2c4e-404e-be27-e159354b9154","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","root":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":null,"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[],"visibility_cache":[]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"43c9bff0-5654-449b-b7ff-1f9e09bc1808","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
{"parentUuid":"bfa5b249-2036-4423-8842-6561fe913a19","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"7c304424-caf5-48fd-bfdc-5e1da322b9a0","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/tacoda/.claude/skills/voice-check\n\n# Voice check\n\n**`~/.claude/VOICE.md` is the standard and the only source of truth. Read it\nfirst, every time. Do not review from memory of it, and do not restate its rules\nhere.** This file only covers how to run the check.\n\nThis is the authoritative stage of the VOICE.md section 15 chain, and the only\none that rewrites nothing. `humanizer` handles tone and `slopbeth` handles slop,\nbut both rewrite, so both can hand back what this skill already rejected. That\nis why it runs first to set the constraints and again at the end as the gate.\n\nIt also catches what neither of them can: Ian's own formula, and drift between\npieces.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read `~/.claude/VOICE.md`.\n2. Run the checker:\n   ```bash\n   python3 ~/.claude/skills/voice-check/voicecheck.py PATH \\\n     --corpus ~/tacoda/tacoda.github.io\n   ```\n   `PATH` takes a file or a directory. HTML is stripped to prose; code blocks,\n   styles and scripts are excluded. `--corpus` enables recycled-phrase detection\n   against the published body of work. Exits non-zero when there are findings.\n3. Add `--show-flow 2` to print the paragraphs where sentences stop connecting.\n   That is the fastest way to fix what reads as cold.\n4. Run `humanizer` for tone, then `slopbeth` for slop, then this checker again\n   as the gate. Both rewriters run under the findings from step 2 as hard\n   constraints: neither may reintroduce a banned phrase, an em dash, or a tic\n   already over budget, and neither may add a claim, number, or first-person\n   experience the draft does not already contain. A finding that reappears at\n   the gate gets fixed in the rewriter that put it back.\n5. **Read the prose.** The script reports signals, not judgment. It cannot see a\n   section that answers no reader question, an aphorism doing the work a\n   mechanism should do, or a missing concession.\n6. Report or fix, per what was asked, quoting the exact span for each finding.\n\n## Reading the output\n\nFindings marked `[advisory]` are the deliberate-structure cases in VOICE.md §5:\nparallel rubric labels, non-goals lists, data triads. They usually mean leave it\nalone. Everything else is a real finding.\n\nTwo metrics are easy to misread:\n\n- `reader address` counts \"you\"/\"your\" **and** imperatives, so a page of\n  instructions scores correctly without contorting into second person.\n- `flow` is measured only inside paragraphs of three or more sentences. A\n  reference page made of tables and captions is not scored on it.\n- `over length` counts body prose only, so a page sitting near the 4,500-word cap\n  is longer than that in wall-clock time once its code blocks and tables are\n  read. VOICE.md §16 says what to cut and in what order.\n\n## Rules\n\n- **Preserve every fact.** Numbers, counts, dates, repo names, stated\n  uncertainty, and non-goals survive any rewrite.\n- **Never invent** first-person experience to hit the \"I\" floor. That floor is a\n  request for a fact only Ian has. Ask him for it.\n- **Do not trade one formula for another.** Replacing negation-definitions with\n  clipped aphorisms is not a fix, and splitting a joined clause into two flat\n  sentences to kill an em dash makes the prose worse, not better.\n- **Over-editing strong prose is a failure.** Targets are ranges, not gates. A\n  deliberate violation that serves the reader is fine; say why in the report.\n\n\nARGUMENTS: /Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion-gh-pages/index.html"}]},"isMeta":true,"turnCompanion":true,"uuid":"f54f9e67-b2f8-4846-ab89-e403206ab4d4","timestamp":"2026-11-11T03:42:46.610Z","sourceToolUseID":"toolu_012k8JvaoAiys3LG9bvyESh1","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
