{"parentUuid":"0ef1c874-454b-4ff0-af45-d2e454700d43","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgvThjDT1Hk6Yb9s6JiiU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The app still carries the quarantine mark, and Gatekeeper rejects it. It's signed ad hoc only, with no team identifier. The cask's step that clears the mark didn't run: you most likely installed from the cask as it was before I pushed that step.\n\n**In System Settings:**\n1. Open System Settings → **Privacy & Security**.\n2. Scroll down to **Security**. Right after the blocked attempt, it shows **\"Bastion\" was blocked to protect your Mac**.\n3. Click **Open Anyway**, then confirm with your password or Touch ID.\n4. Open Bastion again, and choose **Open** in the dialog that follows.\n\nThe button appears only for about an hour after a blocked open. On recent macOS versions, right-click → Open no longer offers to bypass Gatekeeper, so Settings is the way.\n\n**Or from the terminal,** which does what the cask's step was meant to do:\n```\n! xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine /Applications/Bastion.app\n```\n\n**Security note:** either way, you tell macOS to trust an app Apple hasn't checked. That's reasonable here, because you build and publish it, and its download matched the signed checksum. Only a Developer ID signature with notarization removes this step for other users.\n\nFor later installs, `brew reinstall --cask tacoda/bastion/bastion` uses the updated cask, which clears the mark itself."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":562,"cache_read_input_tokens":860963,"output_tokens":649,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":192},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":562,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_toke