{"parentUuid":"254839e2-0e54-4ece-a792-a5aaad242bfc","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"305cbff3-a896-4b01-84bb-b6b3038a90f7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   - Bastion is a Rust software factory: a server on iii plus an iced client, a CLI, and a tray. Work follows PLAN.md phases F1–F11; F1–F10 are done and recorded in PLAN.md.\n   - The user's flow across this session:\n     - \"continue with F7\", then F8, F9, F10 and F11.\n     - A mid-way \"simplify the codebase\" pass.\n     - Distribution: Homebrew plus `curl … | sh`. The script can install the server alone or server + desktop. The server can be installed on any Linux VM/VPS. Update the gh-pages site once a release can be installed.\n     - Relax the default workflows: editable, plus easy to create a new one.\n     - An app icon: a castle in the gh-pages style.\n     - A tray icon to manage the factory with the window closed. It must be white on macOS.\n     - \"push and run a real release\", then \"push and continue with F11\".\n   - Standing rules:\n     - No AI attribution or Co-Authored-By lines anywhere. This overrides the harness reminder.\n     - Run the CodeScene `pre_commit_code_health_safeguard` before each commit, and commit only when it passes.\n     - Stage only your own files, by path.\n     - Push only when asked.\n     - Before a push, run all CI steps: `cargo fmt --all -- --check`, `scripts/check-seams.sh`, `cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`, `cargo test --workspace`.\n     - `bastion-core` may depend only on serde and sha2.\n     - Keep the \"factory\" terminology.\n     - Use `CARGO_INCREMENTAL=0`.\n     - Respond in STE (simplified technical English); the caveman and ponytail hooks are also active.\n     - Published prose follows ~/.claude/VOICE.md; run voice-check as the gate.\n   - Security constraints (verbatim and active):\n     - \"local environments don't need tokens unless they can be automatically setup - credentials are required for remote connections\"\n     - Key values must never pass through the assistant. Never print key values; only masked hints may be shown. The minisign private key stays with the user.\n     - Never list processes with full arguments (`pgrep -fl`, `ps aux`). The iii VM boot lines carry host secrets. Use `ps -o pid=,lstart=,comm=` only.\n     - The auto-mode classifier has denied some actions: killing processes in the past, and deleting or moving a remote git tag. Do not work around denials; ask the user to run `! <command>`. (The user explicitly asked me to stop the dev server; TERM worked.)\n     - Never read or print tokens. Use `/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-tacoda-tacoda-bastion/4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e/scratchpad/api.sh METHOD PATH [JSON]` (it appends an \"HTTP code\" line; strip it with `sed '$d'`). Also use `waitjob.sh <id>` there.\n   - User feedback memory: \"only dark gets to continue unsupervised\". Claude may act on the user's behalf on **test jobs**, but must name each action. api.sh actions are recorded in the audit as \"by Ian Johnson\".\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - Core state machine\n     - `bastion_core::step`, with Event and Effect.\n     - `by_person` handles Retried, Extended and PartsSet.\n     - `approved()` splits scope by `proposed(job)`.\n     - Parts are kept in `job.results[\"parts\"]`.\n     - `Stage::Uat` is a read-only turn after Comprehend. It always waits for a person (forced LightsOn), and only the requester may approve it (`access::approve` / `signs_off`).\n   - Notices (F9)\n     - `on_change: Arc<dyn Fn(Change)>`.\n     - `Change::Notice { job, workspace, state, text, waiting }`.\n     - `notice::entered` and `notice::text`.\n   - GitHub forge\n     - `forge::Notes`.\n     - `describe(job, base, head, &notes)` gives Plan, Briefing, UAT, Proof and Waived sections.\n     - `GitHub::edit`, `deployment(filter)`, `merge_commit`.\n     - Deploy watch: `deploy_outcome`, with NO_DEPLOY_POLLS = 6.\n   - Escapes\n     - `factory/reverts.rs`: `lands()` covers Land, Ship and Merge; also `landed_in`, `note_escape` and `sweep_reverts`.\n     - `escape::reverts` parses `git log --format=%H%x1f%B%x1e`.\n   - Requests\n     - `bastion_core::request::compose`, with KINDS feature, bug, chore and docs.\n     - Screenshots go to `/work/request` in the VM. The host copy is at `scratch/requests/<root job>`, with names kept in `results[\"screenshots\"]`.\n   - Store layout\n     - `store/{schema,sql,fake,tests,live}.rs`.\n     - `GovernStore` covers dials, constraints, capabilities, held calls and audit.\n     - Columns are added through ALTERS (currently 23): jobs.model, eval_runs.contract, workspaces.image.\n   - Layout (F10)\n     - The `bastion_client::start::Layout{server, stack, data}` struct.\n     - An installed build finds its stack at `exe_dir/../share/bastion`, after canonicalizing current_exe, and keeps its data in the user's data folder.\n     - The pointer file `config_dir/local-server` holds only the data directory path.\n     - `search_path` adds `~/.local/bin`, `/opt/homebrew/bin` and `/usr/local/bin`.\n   - Release\n     - `scripts/package.sh` builds `bastion-<v>-<target>.tar.gz` and `bastion-desktop-<v>-<target>.zip` (Bastion.app, ad-hoc codesigned) or a Linux tarball.\n     - `.github/workflows/release.yml` runs on v* tags with a matrix of macos-latest, ubuntu-24.04 and ubuntu-24.04-arm. It signs SHA256SUMS with minisign (secret MINISIGN_SECRET_KEY) and publishes with `gh release create`.\n     - `scripts/install.sh` supports `--desktop`, `--service` (systemd user unit), `--version` and `--prefix`.\n     - `bastion update` uses the `Release{tag,target}` struct, minisign-verify, sha2, and `/releases/latest` redirect reading.\n     - `scripts/brew.sh` writes Formula/bastion.rb and Casks/bastion.rb.\n     - The cask depends on the formula, links `bastion-desktop`, and has a postflight `xattr -dr com.apple.quarantine`.\n   - Tray\n     - The `bastion-tray` crate uses tao 0.37 and tray-icon 0.26.\n     - `menu.rs` turns a Snapshot into entries; `worker.rs` holds the client link.\n     - It holds a lock file at `config_dir/tray.lock`.\n     - The desktop starts the tray, logging to `tray.log`.\n   - F11\n     - The `browser` custom image is `mcr.microsoft.com/playwright:v1.65.0-noble`, registered in worker-compose.yaml via `custom_images` and `image_allowlist`.\n     - `Workspace.image` is python, node or browser; `vm_image()`.\n     - The BROWSER_GUIDE text is added to turn messages.\n     - Media: `bastion_core::media`, with DIR `/work/artifacts`, the \"Kept from /work/artifacts: <name>\" artifact, and a 50 MB limit. It is handled by `factory/media.rs keep_media`, called from `tidy_after_turn`.\n     - Media are served at `GET /jobs/{id}/media/{name}` (MEDIA_ROUTE).\n     - Desktop: the `JobDetail.media` field and a \"Kept files\" section with Open.\n\n3. Files and Code Sections (key, mostly committed):\n   - crates/bastion-server/src/factory/media.rs (new, F11b): `listed(answer)`, `media_dir`, `media_path`, `keep_media` (pub(crate)) and `keep_one`.\n   - crates/bastion-core/src/media.rs (new): `kept`, `artifact`, `named`, `content_type`.\n   - crates/bastion-server/src/factory.rs:\n     - `mod media; mod reverts; pub use reverts::ESCAPE; mod later_tests` (test-only).\n     - BROWSER_GUIDE const, added in start_turn when `w.vm_image()==\"browser\"`.\n     - `keep_screenshots` / `put_screenshots`.\n     - `changed()` and `notice()`.\n     - The pump checks intake_paused.\n     - Watches: `watch_deploy`, `check_contract` before teardown.\n   - Current BROWSER_GUIDE text: \"This VM has Playwright with Chromium, Firefox and WebKit, and Node. It has no network, so serve the app inside the VM (for example on 127.0.0.1) and point the browser there. Keep each screenshot (.png) and recording (.webm) you make under /work/artifacts; they are kept with the job for the person to see.\"\n   - crates/bastion-server/src/factory/later_tests.rs: holds the F7+ tests, which use `super::tests::*`. The rig helpers are now pub(super). Rig has a `told` field for recorded Changes.\n   - worker-compose.yaml: `image_allowlist: [python, node, browser]` and `custom_images: browser: mcr.microsoft.com/playwright:v1.65.0-noble`.\n   - scripts/minisign.pub: public key `REDACTED`, also embedded in install.sh.\n   - PLAN.md: the F row reads \"F1 to F10 done\", with notes for F6–F10 and F10's distribution requirements.\n   - The gh-pages worktree is at /Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion-gh-pages. The site is updated (commit 26eecda) and serves install.sh. tacoda.github.io/bastion redirects to www.tacoda.dev/bastion/.\n   - The tap clone is in the scratchpad at homebrew-bastion (latest commit 901ea77).\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - Code Health failures, each fixed by refactoring:\n     - learn view args\n     - gates string-heavy\n     - store low cohesion (split)\n     - doctor\n     - signs_off complex conditional\n     - step complexity (moved into by_person)\n     - http duplication (decide_as/job_decide)\n     - update.rs string-heavy (Release struct)\n     - worker.rs bumpy road (attach/on_action)\n     - factory/tests.rs over 1000 LOC (split into later_tests.rs)\n   - Learn JSON came after the reasoning text, so the reader now finds it within the text. Retry duplicates are handled by reading only the newest reply.\n   - A local land ended the job before learn; the fix sets Landed to advance.\n   - The contract check raced the teardown; the check now runs before teardown.\n   - The judge used OpenAI with no key; it now uses the plan_model.\n   - The macos-14 runner was retired; the workflow now uses macos-latest. Moving the tag was denied by the classifier, and the user moved it.\n   - In brew.sh, an apostrophe in `${:?}` broke the parse. The cask's `conflicts_with formula` and `depends_on macos \">= :ventura\"` were invalid. Homebrew 7 has no `--no-quarantine`, so a postflight xattr was added. The user needed \"Open Anyway\" in System Settings.\n   - The installed app attached to the dev server with a stale token. Fixed with the pointer file, plus asking the user. At the user's request I stopped the dev server (TERM 70580).\n   - Finder launches lacked `iii` on PATH; fixed with search_path.\n   - Disk filled up once (rustc SIGABRT); the user freed space.\n   - F11:\n     - The playwright image's npm package is removed; only the browsers remain.\n     - In `network:false` VMs the loopback interface `lo` is DOWN (\"Network is unreachable\" for 127.0.0.1), so Chromium gives ERR_INTERNET_DISCONNECTED.\n     - There is no `ip` command, but Perl's ioctl SIOCSIFFLAGS brings lo up. After that, a served page was screenshotted successfully (served.png, 8080 bytes).\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - Verified live:\n     - F7 PR #1 on ianparo/bastion-demo, including deploy \"Nothing deployed\" and the revert escape.\n     - F8: pause, model, details, split and workflow reset.\n     - F9: notification, tray count, Approve.\n     - F10 release v0.1.0: signature verified, install.sh worked, update reported \"is the newest\", desktop install worked, brew formula installed.\n   - F11 so far: the browser VM boot took about 9 minutes on first pull, leaving about 3 GiB free. Also verified git 2.43, node 26, ffmpeg and /ms-playwright browsers. A file:// screenshot works. A served page works only after bringing lo up via Perl.\n   - Lingering processes:\n     - The old installed bastion-server, PID 94604, did not exit after TERM. The user can run `! kill -9 94604`.\n     - The dev server now runs (PID 2198, from target/debug, data in repo data/server). The pointer is written to the repo's data/server.\n     - Test sandbox 7ac5f38a-74b5-444b-bb06-778cd6e64013 is still up.\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - \"Did we update the product site yet?\"\n   - \"continue with F8\"\n   - \"I also need the curl install methods to be able to install the server or both the server and desktop app\"\n   - \"the bastion-server should be able to be installed on any virtual machine or vps\"\n   - \"After it's deployed and ready to install, I need to update the gh-pages site. It is out of date.\"\n   - \"I also want to relax the default workflows - I want a good amount of starting workflows, but I want the user to be able to change them and also to easily create a new one\"\n   - \"restarted, continue\" (several times)\n   - \"push\"\n   - \"continue with F9\"\n   - \"I also want to create an icon for this app. Generate one for me that is a castle, similar in style to what is on the gh-pages site\"\n   - \"continue\" (after the disk was freed)\n   - \"I also want an icon in the tray that I can use to manage my factory. I want to be able to close the app, keep the server running, and use the tray icon to check up on what's happening and even interact with the factory at a basic level\"\n   - \"I don't want the tray icon to be black like in windows / I want the tray icon to be white on macos\"\n   - \"The notification popped up / restarted, continue\"\n   - \"The app icon still is not correct\" (image of the generic exec Dock icon)\n   - \"push and continue\"\n   - Answers to questions: unsigned macOS for now; a minisign key; \"Yes, I'll create it\" (the tap); \"I'll make the key first\"\n   - \"push and run a real release\"\n   - \"Commands run\"\n   - \"The macos job failed\" (image: macos-14 unsupported)\n   - \"It installed bastion and bastion-server, but I don't see bastion-desktop in my path\"\n   - \"I get an error with no flag for no-quarantine\"\n   - \"It doesn't open - how do I allow it in settings?\"\n   - \"It opens now, continue with F11 / Also, the server didn't start with the app - I expected the desktop to automatically start the server if it wasn't already running\"\n   - \"I don't know where the dev server is. Shut it down so I can start it with the desktop app\"\n   - \"the app started, but the desktop app did not start\"\n   - \"Just came up\"\n   - \"That was a very long startup time - was it because it was waiting for the server to start?\"\n   - \"push and continue with F11\"\n   - F11 answers: \"Microsoft's Playwright image\"; \"Now, after I free disk space\"\n   - \"I freed up the space\"\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Finish F11:\n     - The factory brings `lo` up in a VM when it boots, at least for the browser image. Use the Perl ioctl snippet, or another tool present in the image.\n     - Update BROWSER_GUIDE with the tested offline paths: chrome-headless-shell `--no-sandbox --screenshot`, file://. Playwright scripts and recordings need the playwright npm package from the workspace setup.\n     - Run one browser job end to end, checking that kept media appear on the job page.\n     - Record F11 in PLAN.md, then commit and push (the user said \"push and continue with F11\").\n   - Later: release v0.1.1 so the installed app gets the pointer fix and the PATH fix (949d25a, 2a3eaa5), if the user wants that.\n   - Optional fix noted in code: the pointer's staleness (try its token and fall back).\n\n8. Current Work:\n   I was live-testing F11 in the booted browser sandbox `7ac5f38a-74b5-444b-bb06-778cd6e64013`. I found that loopback is down in offline VMs. Bringing it up with this Perl script (base64-copied to `/tmp/loup.pl` and run with `perl`) made the server reachable:\n   ```perl\n   use Socket;\n   socket(my $s, AF_INET, SOCK_DGRAM, 0) or die \"socket: $!\";\n   my $SIOCGIFFLAGS = 0x8913; my $SIOCSIFFLAGS = 0x8914; my $IFF_UP = 0x1;\n   my $req = pack('a16 s', 'lo', 0);\n   ioctl($s, $SIOCGIFFLAGS, $req) or die \"get: $!\";\n   my (undef, $flags) = unpack('a16 s', $req);\n   $req = pack('a16 s', 'lo', $flags | $IFF_UP);\n   ioctl($s, $SIOCSIFFLAGS, $req) or die \"set: $!\";\n   print \"lo up\\n\";\n   ```\n   After that, a Node server on 127.0.0.1:8002 answered curl. `/ms-playwright/chromium_headless_shell-*/chrome-headless-shell-linux*/chrome-headless-shell --no-sandbox --window-size=800,400 --screenshot=/work/artifacts/served.png http://127.0.0.1:8002/` returned rc=0 and served.png was 8080 bytes. A server and the browser must run in the same exec, because background processes don't survive past their exec. F11a (de0471a) and F11b (e1ce75e) are committed but not pushed; origin/main is at 2a3eaa5.\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   Continue F11, which the user asked for (\"push and continue with F11\"):\n   - Make the factory bring loopback up after it provisions a VM. For example, in setup's fill or provision, exec the Perl ioctl one-liner (perl exists in the Ubuntu-based images), and treat a failure as harmless.\n   - Revise BROWSER_GUIDE to name the working headless-shell command and the same-exec rule for local servers.\n   - Commit, with the safeguard and a test.\n   - Then run one browser-image job on a test workspace set to `image: browser` to confirm the kept media show on the job page.\n   - Record F11 in PLAN.md, run all CI steps, and push.\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /Users/tacoda/.claude/projects/-Users-tacoda-tacoda-bastion/4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened."},"isVisibleInTranscriptOnly":true,"isCompactSummary":true,"uuid":"c6af1b4d-33bc-4caa-b183-35c49a7f64db","timestamp":"2026-11-11T04:26:12.500Z","turnPosition":{"promptIndex":143,"turnIndex":229},"turnOrigin":"task_notification","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
{"parentUuid":"c6af1b4d-33bc-4caa-b183-35c49a7f64db","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-tacoda-tacoda-bastion/4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e/tasks/b26hph442.output","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-tacoda-tacoda-bastion/4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e/tasks/b26hph442.output","content":"Tue Nov 10 22:10:47 CST 2026\nTue Nov 10 22:19:58 CST 2026\n{\n  \"image\": \"browser\",\n  \"sandbox_id\": \"7ac5f38a-74b5-444b-bb06-778cd6e64013\"\n}\n3.0Gi free\n\n[exited with code 0]\n","numLines":10,"startLine":1,"totalLines":10}},"displayPath":"../../../../private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-tacoda-tacoda-bastion/4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e/tasks/b26hph442.output"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"1117aebe-2199-4b5f-9650-8fe695c50c66","timestamp":"2026-11-11T04:26:12.701Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-tacoda-tacoda-bastion/4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e/tasks/b26hph442.output\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\tTue Nov 10 22:10:47 CST 2026\n2\tTue Nov 10 22:19:58 CST 2026\n3\t{\n4\t  \"image\": \"browser\",\n5\t  \"sandbox_id\": \"7ac5f38a-74b5-444b-bb06-778cd6e64013\"\n6\t}\n7\t3.0Gi free\n8\t\n9\t[exited with code 0]\n10\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
{"parentUuid":"1117aebe-2199-4b5f-9650-8fe695c50c66","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/crates/bastion-server/src/factory/media.rs","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/crates/bastion-server/src/factory/media.rs","content":"//! Screenshots and recordings (F11): after a turn, each image or recording\n//! the turn left under /work/artifacts in its VM is copied to the host and\n//! recorded on the job, once.\n\nuse std::path::PathBuf;\n\nuse bastion_core::media;\nuse serde_json::{Value, json};\n\nuse super::{Factory, SLOW_MS, now};\nuse crate::engine::DEFAULT_NAMESPACE;\nuse crate::store::{Artifact, Record};\n\n/// The largest file kept, so a stray recording cannot fill the disk.\nconst LARGEST: u64 = 50 * 1024 * 1024;\n\n/// The kept-kind files a `sandbox::fs::ls` answer lists, small enough to keep.\nfn listed(answer: &Value) -> Vec<String> {\n    let entries = answer[\"entries\"].as_array().cloned().unwrap_or_default();\n    entries\n        .iter()\n        .filter(|e| e[\"is_dir\"] != true && e[\"size\"].as_u64().unwrap_or(0) <= LARGEST)\n        .filter_map(|e| e[\"name\"].as_str().map(String::from))\n        .filter(|n| media::kept(n))\n        .collect()\n}\n\nimpl Factory {\n    /// Where a job's kept files are on the host.\n    fn media_dir(&self, job: &str) -> PathBuf {\n        self.scratch.join(\"media\").join(job)\n    }\n\n    /// The host path of `name` among `job`'s kept files, if it is one.\n    pub fn media_path(&self, job: &str, name: &str) -> Option<PathBuf> {\n        let path = self.media_dir(job).join(name);\n        (media::kept(name) && self.machine.exists(&path)).then_some(path)\n    }\n\n    /// Copy out what the turn kept under /work/artifacts that the job does\n    /// not hold yet. A file that cannot be read is left for the next turn.\n    pub(crate) async fn keep_media(&self, record: &Record) {\n        let Some(vm) = &record.runtime.sandbox else {\n            return;\n        };\n        let ask = json!({ \"sandbox_id\": vm, \"path\": media::DIR });\n        let Ok(answer) = self\n            .engine\n            .call(DEFAULT_NAMESPACE, \"sandbox::fs::ls\", ask)\n            .await\n        else {\n            return;\n        };\n        let id = &record.job.id;\n        let held = self.store.artifacts(id).await.unwrap_or_default();\n        let have: Vec<&str> = held.iter().filter_map(|a| media::named(&a.body)).collect();\n        for name in listed(&answer)\n            .into_iter()\n            .filter(|n| !have.contains(&n.as_str()))\n        {\n            if let Err(e) = self.keep_one(record, vm, &name).await {\n                eprintln!(\"job {id}: {name} was not kept: {e}\");\n            }\n        }\n    }\n\n    async fn keep_one(&self, record: &Record, vm: &str, name: &str) -> Result<(), String> {\n        let path = format!(\"{}/{name}\", media::DIR);\n        let ask = json!({ \"sandbox_id\": vm, \"path\": path });\n        let read = self\n            .engine\n            .call_for(DEFAULT_NAMESPACE, \"sandbox::fs::read\", ask, SLOW_MS);\n        let bytes = self\n            .engine\n            .read_channel(read.await?[\"content\"].clone())\n            .await?;\n        let dir = self.media_dir(&record.job.id);\n        self.machine.make_dir(&dir)?;\n        self.machine.write_bytes(&dir.join(name), &bytes)?;\n        let kept = Artifact {\n            stage: record.job.stage,\n            body: media::artifact(name),\n            created_at: now(),\n        };\n        self.store.add_artifact(&record.job.id, &kept).await\n    }\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n\n    #[test]\n    fn a_listing_keeps_images_and_recordings_under_the_size_limit() {\n        let answer = json!({ \"entries\": [\n            { \"name\": \"home.png\", \"is_dir\": false, \"size\": 2048 },\n            { \"name\": \"flow.webm\", \"is_dir\": false, \"size\": 4096 },\n            { \"name\": \"notes.txt\", \"is_dir\": false, \"size\": 10 },\n            { \"name\": \"shots\", \"is_dir\": true, \"size\": 0 },\n            { \"name\": \"huge.mp4\", \"is_dir\": false, \"size\": LARGEST + 1 },\n        ]});\n        assert_eq!(listed(&answer), [\"home.png\", \"flow.webm\"]);\n        assert!(listed(&json!({})).is_empty());\n    }\n}\n","numLines":106,"startLine":1,"totalLines":106}},"displayPath":"crates/bastion-server/src/factory/media.rs"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"ed3bfe50-2d81-44e3-b99e-3de85b291a8d","timestamp":"2026-11-11T04:26:12.701Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/crates/bastion-server/src/factory/media.rs\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t//! Screenshots and recordings (F11): after a turn, each image or recording\n2\t//! the turn left under /work/artifacts in its VM is copied to the host and\n3\t//! recorded on the job, once.\n4\t\n5\tuse std::path::PathBuf;\n6\t\n7\tuse bastion_core::media;\n8\tuse serde_json::{Value, json};\n9\t\n10\tuse super::{Factory, SLOW_MS, now};\n11\tuse crate::engine::DEFAULT_NAMESPACE;\n12\tuse crate::store::{Artifact, Record};\n13\t\n14\t/// The largest file kept, so a stray recording cannot fill the disk.\n15\tconst LARGEST: u64 = 50 * 1024 * 1024;\n16\t\n17\t/// The kept-kind files a `sandbox::fs::ls` answer lists, small enough to keep.\n18\tfn listed(answer: &Value) -> Vec<String> {\n19\t    let entries = answer[\"entries\"].as_array().cloned().unwrap_or_default();\n20\t    entries\n21\t        .iter()\n22\t        .filter(|e| e[\"is_dir\"] != true && e[\"size\"].as_u64().unwrap_or(0) <= LARGEST)\n23\t        .filter_map(|e| e[\"name\"].as_str().map(String::from))\n24\t        .filter(|n| media::kept(n))\n25\t        .collect()\n26\t}\n27\t\n28\timpl Factory {\n29\t    /// Where a job's kept files are on the host.\n30\t    fn media_dir(&self, job: &str) -> PathBuf {\n31\t        self.scratch.join(\"media\").join(job)\n32\t    }\n33\t\n34\t    /// The host path of `name` among `job`'s kept files, if it is one.\n35\t    pub fn media_path(&self, job: &str, name: &str) -> Option<PathBuf> {\n36\t        let path = self.media_dir(job).join(name);\n37\t        (media::kept(name) && self.machine.exists(&path)).then_some(path)\n38\t    }\n39\t\n40\t    /// Copy out what the turn kept under /work/artifacts that the job does\n41\t    /// not hold yet. A file that cannot be read is left for the next turn.\n42\t    pub(crate) async fn keep_media(&self, record: &Record) {\n43\t        let Some(vm) = &record.runtime.sandbox else {\n44\t            return;\n45\t        };\n46\t        let ask = json!({ \"sandbox_id\": vm, \"path\": media::DIR });\n47\t        let Ok(answer) = self\n48\t            .engine\n49\t            .call(DEFAULT_NAMESPACE, \"sandbox::fs::ls\", ask)\n50\t            .await\n51\t        else {\n52\t            return;\n53\t        };\n54\t        let id = &record.job.id;\n55\t        let held = self.store.artifacts(id).await.unwrap_or_default();\n56\t        let have: Vec<&str> = held.iter().filter_map(|a| media::named(&a.body)).collect();\n57\t        for name in listed(&answer)\n58\t            .into_iter()\n59\t            .filter(|n| !have.contains(&n.as_str()))\n60\t        {\n61\t            if let Err(e) = self.keep_one(record, vm, &name).await {\n62\t                eprintln!(\"job {id}: {name} was not kept: {e}\");\n63\t            }\n64\t        }\n65\t    }\n66\t\n67\t    async fn keep_one(&self, record: &Record, vm: &str, name: &str) -> Result<(), String> {\n68\t        let path = format!(\"{}/{name}\", media::DIR);\n69\t        let ask = json!({ \"sandbox_id\": vm, \"path\": path });\n70\t        let read = self\n71\t            .engine\n72\t            .call_for(DEFAULT_NAMESPACE, \"sandbox::fs::read\", ask, SLOW_MS);\n73\t        let bytes = self\n74\t            .engine\n75\t            .read_channel(read.await?[\"content\"].clone())\n76\t            .await?;\n77\t        let dir = self.media_dir(&record.job.id);\n78\t        self.machine.make_dir(&dir)?;\n79\t        self.machine.write_bytes(&dir.join(name), &bytes)?;\n80\t        let kept = Artifact {\n81\t            stage: record.job.stage,\n82\t            body: media::artifact(name),\n83\t            created_at: now(),\n84\t        };\n85\t        self.store.add_artifact(&record.job.id, &kept).await\n86\t    }\n87\t}\n88\t\n89\t#[cfg(test)]\n90\tmod tests {\n91\t    use super::*;\n92\t\n93\t    #[test]\n94\t    fn a_listing_keeps_images_and_recordings_under_the_size_limit() {\n95\t        let answer = json!({ \"entries\": [\n96\t            { \"name\": \"home.png\", \"is_dir\": false, \"size\": 2048 },\n97\t            { \"name\": \"flow.webm\", \"is_dir\": false, \"size\": 4096 },\n98\t            { \"name\": \"notes.txt\", \"is_dir\": false, \"size\": 10 },\n99\t            { \"name\": \"shots\", \"is_dir\": true, \"size\": 0 },\n100\t            { \"name\": \"huge.mp4\", \"is_dir\": false, \"size\": LARGEST + 1 },\n101\t        ]});\n102\t        assert_eq!(listed(&answer), [\"home.png\", \"flow.webm\"]);\n103\t        assert!(listed(&json!({})).is_empty());\n104\t    }\n105\t}\n106\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
{"parentUuid":"ed3bfe50-2d81-44e3-b99e-3de85b291a8d","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/crates/bastion-core/src/media.rs","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/crates/bastion-core/src/media.rs","content":"//! Screenshots and recordings a turn keeps (F11): files under\n//! /work/artifacts in its VM, copied out after the turn and recorded on the\n//! job, one artifact line each.\n\n/// Where a turn keeps the files it wants a person to see.\npub const DIR: &str = \"/work/artifacts\";\n\n/// How an artifact names a kept file.\nconst KEPT: &str = \"Kept from /work/artifacts: \";\n\n/// The kinds of file kept, by extension: images and recordings.\nconst KINDS: [&str; 7] = [\"png\", \"jpg\", \"jpeg\", \"gif\", \"webp\", \"webm\", \"mp4\"];\n\n/// Whether `name` is a plain file name of a kind kept.\npub fn kept(name: &str) -> bool {\n    let plain = !name.is_empty() && !name.contains(['/', '\\\\']) && !name.starts_with('.');\n    let ext = name.rsplit_once('.').map(|(_, e)| e.to_ascii_lowercase());\n    plain && ext.is_some_and(|e| KINDS.contains(&e.as_str()))\n}\n\n/// The artifact that records a kept file.\npub fn artifact(name: &str) -> String {\n    format!(\"{KEPT}{name}\")\n}\n\n/// The kept file an artifact records, if it records one.\npub fn named(body: &str) -> Option<&str> {\n    body.strip_prefix(KEPT).filter(|n| kept(n))\n}\n\n/// The content type a kept file is served with.\npub fn content_type(name: &str) -> &'static str {\n    match name\n        .rsplit_once('.')\n        .map(|(_, e)| e.to_ascii_lowercase())\n        .as_deref()\n    {\n        Some(\"png\") => \"image/png\",\n        Some(\"jpg\" | \"jpeg\") => \"image/jpeg\",\n        Some(\"gif\") => \"image/gif\",\n        Some(\"webp\") => \"image/webp\",\n        Some(\"webm\") => \"video/webm\",\n        Some(\"mp4\") => \"video/mp4\",\n        _ => \"application/octet-stream\",\n    }\n}\n\n#[cfg(test)]\nmod tests {\n    use super::*;\n\n    #[test]\n    fn images_and_recordings_are_kept_by_their_plain_names() {\n        assert!(kept(\"home.png\") && kept(\"login flow.WEBM\"));\n        for bad in [\"notes.txt\", \"../x.png\", \"a/b.png\", \".png\", \"\"] {\n            assert!(!kept(bad), \"{bad}\");\n        }\n        assert_eq!(named(&artifact(\"home.png\")), Some(\"home.png\"));\n        assert_eq!(named(\"Kept from /work/artifacts: ../x.png\"), None);\n        assert_eq!(named(\"Plan: home.png\"), None);\n        assert_eq!(content_type(\"run.webm\"), \"video/webm\");\n    }\n}\n","numLines":64,"startLine":1,"totalLines":64}},"displayPath":"crates/bastion-core/src/media.rs"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"6fc87c39-b4bc-4f6c-aa15-64c567961266","timestamp":"2026-11-11T04:26:12.701Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/crates/bastion-core/src/media.rs\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t//! Screenshots and recordings a turn keeps (F11): files under\n2\t//! /work/artifacts in its VM, copied out after the turn and recorded on the\n3\t//! job, one artifact line each.\n4\t\n5\t/// Where a turn keeps the files it wants a person to see.\n6\tpub const DIR: &str = \"/work/artifacts\";\n7\t\n8\t/// How an artifact names a kept file.\n9\tconst KEPT: &str = \"Kept from /work/artifacts: \";\n10\t\n11\t/// The kinds of file kept, by extension: images and recordings.\n12\tconst KINDS: [&str; 7] = [\"png\", \"jpg\", \"jpeg\", \"gif\", \"webp\", \"webm\", \"mp4\"];\n13\t\n14\t/// Whether `name` is a plain file name of a kind kept.\n15\tpub fn kept(name: &str) -> bool {\n16\t    let plain = !name.is_empty() && !name.contains(['/', '\\\\']) && !name.starts_with('.');\n17\t    let ext = name.rsplit_once('.').map(|(_, e)| e.to_ascii_lowercase());\n18\t    plain && ext.is_some_and(|e| KINDS.contains(&e.as_str()))\n19\t}\n20\t\n21\t/// The artifact that records a kept file.\n22\tpub fn artifact(name: &str) -> String {\n23\t    format!(\"{KEPT}{name}\")\n24\t}\n25\t\n26\t/// The kept file an artifact records, if it records one.\n27\tpub fn named(body: &str) -> Option<&str> {\n28\t    body.strip_prefix(KEPT).filter(|n| kept(n))\n29\t}\n30\t\n31\t/// The content type a kept file is served with.\n32\tpub fn content_type(name: &str) -> &'static str {\n33\t    match name\n34\t        .rsplit_once('.')\n35\t        .map(|(_, e)| e.to_ascii_lowercase())\n36\t        .as_deref()\n37\t    {\n38\t        Some(\"png\") => \"image/png\",\n39\t        Some(\"jpg\" | \"jpeg\") => \"image/jpeg\",\n40\t        Some(\"gif\") => \"image/gif\",\n41\t        Some(\"webp\") => \"image/webp\",\n42\t        Some(\"webm\") => \"video/webm\",\n43\t        Some(\"mp4\") => \"video/mp4\",\n44\t        _ => \"application/octet-stream\",\n45\t    }\n46\t}\n47\t\n48\t#[cfg(test)]\n49\tmod tests {\n50\t    use super::*;\n51\t\n52\t    #[test]\n53\t    fn images_and_recordings_are_kept_by_their_plain_names() {\n54\t        assert!(kept(\"home.png\") && kept(\"login flow.WEBM\"));\n55\t        for bad in [\"notes.txt\", \"../x.png\", \"a/b.png\", \".png\", \"\"] {\n56\t            assert!(!kept(bad), \"{bad}\");\n57\t        }\n58\t        assert_eq!(named(&artifact(\"home.png\")), Some(\"home.png\"));\n59\t        assert_eq!(named(\"Kept from /work/artifacts: ../x.png\"), None);\n60\t        assert_eq!(named(\"Plan: home.png\"), None);\n61\t        assert_eq!(content_type(\"run.webm\"), \"video/webm\");\n62\t    }\n63\t}\n64\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
{"parentUuid":"6fc87c39-b4bc-4f6c-aa15-64c567961266","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"compact_file_reference","filename":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/crates/bastion-core/src/lib.rs","displayPath":"crates/bastion-core/src/lib.rs"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"50ee55b2-1e38-44e9-bb1e-b273a1bd23a3","timestamp":"2026-11-11T04:26:12.701Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/crates/bastion-core/src/lib.rs was read before the last conversation was summarized, but the contents are too large to include. Use Read tool if you need to access it.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
{"parentUuid":"50ee55b2-1e38-44e9-bb1e-b273a1bd23a3","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"file","filename":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/scripts/brew.sh","content":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/scripts/brew.sh","content":"#!/usr/bin/env bash\n# Write the Homebrew tap's formula and cask for a release (PLAN.md F10):\n#   Formula/bastion.rb  the server and the CLI, with their stack\n#   Casks/bastion.rb    Bastion.app, on the formula, with bastion-desktop on the path\n# Usage: scripts/brew.sh <version> <SHA256SUMS> <tap checkout>\nset -euo pipefail\n\nversion=\"${1:?usage: scripts/brew.sh <version> <SHA256SUMS> <tap>}\"\nsums=\"${2:?the SHA256SUMS of the release}\"\ntap=\"${3:?a checkout of the tap}\"\nv=\"${version#v}\"\nurl=\"https://github.com/tacoda/bastion/releases/download/v$v\"\n\nsum() {\n  local found\n  found=\"$(grep \" $1\\$\" \"$sums\" | cut -d ' ' -f 1)\"\n  [ -n \"$found\" ] || { echo \"$1 is not in $sums\" >&2; exit 1; }\n  echo \"$found\"\n}\n\nmkdir -p \"$tap/Formula\" \"$tap/Casks\"\n\ncat > \"$tap/Formula/bastion.rb\" <<EOF\nclass Bastion < Formula\n  desc \"Software factory: agents plan, build and land changes under governance\"\n  homepage \"https://tacoda.github.io/bastion/\"\n  version \"$v\"\n\n  on_macos do\n    on_arm do\n      url \"$url/bastion-$v-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz\"\n      sha256 \"$(sum \"bastion-$v-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz\")\"\n    end\n  end\n\n  on_linux do\n    on_intel do\n      url \"$url/bastion-$v-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz\"\n      sha256 \"$(sum \"bastion-$v-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz\")\"\n    end\n    on_arm do\n      url \"$url/bastion-$v-aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz\"\n      sha256 \"$(sum \"bastion-$v-aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz\")\"\n    end\n  end\n\n  def install\n    # The stack stays beside the binaries, where they look for it.\n    libexec.install \"bin\", \"share\"\n    bin.install_symlink libexec/\"bin/bastion\", libexec/\"bin/bastion-server\"\n  end\n\n  def caveats\n    <<~TEXT\n      Bastion's stack runs on iii 0.25.0, installed on its own;\n      \\`bastion-server\\` checks for it and says how before it starts.\n    TEXT\n  end\n\n  test do\n    assert_match \"usage: bastion\", shell_output(\"#{bin}/bastion 2>&1\", 1)\n  end\nend\nEOF\n\ncat > \"$tap/Casks/bastion.rb\" <<EOF\ncask \"bastion\" do\n  version \"$v\"\n  sha256 \"$(sum \"bastion-desktop-$v-aarch64-apple-darwin.zip\")\"\n\n  url \"$url/bastion-desktop-#{version}-aarch64-apple-darwin.zip\"\n  name \"Bastion\"\n  desc \"Watch and steer the Bastion software factory\"\n  homepage \"https://tacoda.github.io/bastion/\"\n\n  depends_on arch: :arm64\n  depends_on macos: :ventura\n  # The CLI and the server come from the formula, so they are on the path once.\n  depends_on formula: \"tacoda/bastion/bastion\"\n\n  app \"Bastion.app\"\n  binary \"#{appdir}/Bastion.app/Contents/MacOS/bastion-desktop\"\n\n  # Not signed with a Developer ID yet, and Homebrew dropped --no-quarantine:\n  # the download is pinned to its signed sum, so the quarantine mark goes.\n  postflight do\n    system_command \"/usr/bin/xattr\",\n                   args: [\"-dr\", \"com.apple.quarantine\", \"#{appdir}/Bastion.app\"]\n  end\nend\nEOF\n\necho \"wrote $tap/Formula/bastion.rb and $tap/Casks/bastion.rb for $v\"\n","numLines":94,"startLine":1,"totalLines":94}},"displayPath":"scripts/brew.sh"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"323aedef-623f-498a-ba1a-ee48e8b45ae3","timestamp":"2026-11-11T04:26:12.701Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCalled the Read tool with the following input: {\"file_path\":\"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/scripts/brew.sh\"}\n</system-reminder>"},{"content":"<system-reminder>\nResult of calling the Read tool:\n1\t#!/usr/bin/env bash\n2\t# Write the Homebrew tap's formula and cask for a release (PLAN.md F10):\n3\t#   Formula/bastion.rb  the server and the CLI, with their stack\n4\t#   Casks/bastion.rb    Bastion.app, on the formula, with bastion-desktop on the path\n5\t# Usage: scripts/brew.sh <version> <SHA256SUMS> <tap checkout>\n6\tset -euo pipefail\n7\t\n8\tversion=\"${1:?usage: scripts/brew.sh <version> <SHA256SUMS> <tap>}\"\n9\tsums=\"${2:?the SHA256SUMS of the release}\"\n10\ttap=\"${3:?a checkout of the tap}\"\n11\tv=\"${version#v}\"\n12\turl=\"https://github.com/tacoda/bastion/releases/download/v$v\"\n13\t\n14\tsum() {\n15\t  local found\n16\t  found=\"$(grep \" $1\\$\" \"$sums\" | cut -d ' ' -f 1)\"\n17\t  [ -n \"$found\" ] || { echo \"$1 is not in $sums\" >&2; exit 1; }\n18\t  echo \"$found\"\n19\t}\n20\t\n21\tmkdir -p \"$tap/Formula\" \"$tap/Casks\"\n22\t\n23\tcat > \"$tap/Formula/bastion.rb\" <<EOF\n24\tclass Bastion < Formula\n25\t  desc \"Software factory: agents plan, build and land changes under governance\"\n26\t  homepage \"https://tacoda.github.io/bastion/\"\n27\t  version \"$v\"\n28\t\n29\t  on_macos do\n30\t    on_arm do\n31\t      url \"$url/bastion-$v-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz\"\n32\t      sha256 \"$(sum \"bastion-$v-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz\")\"\n33\t    end\n34\t  end\n35\t\n36\t  on_linux do\n37\t    on_intel do\n38\t      url \"$url/bastion-$v-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz\"\n39\t      sha256 \"$(sum \"bastion-$v-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz\")\"\n40\t    end\n41\t    on_arm do\n42\t      url \"$url/bastion-$v-aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz\"\n43\t      sha256 \"$(sum \"bastion-$v-aarch64-unknown-linux-gnu.tar.gz\")\"\n44\t    end\n45\t  end\n46\t\n47\t  def install\n48\t    # The stack stays beside the binaries, where they look for it.\n49\t    libexec.install \"bin\", \"share\"\n50\t    bin.install_symlink libexec/\"bin/bastion\", libexec/\"bin/bastion-server\"\n51\t  end\n52\t\n53\t  def caveats\n54\t    <<~TEXT\n55\t      Bastion's stack runs on iii 0.25.0, installed on its own;\n56\t      \\`bastion-server\\` checks for it and says how before it starts.\n57\t    TEXT\n58\t  end\n59\t\n60\t  test do\n61\t    assert_match \"usage: bastion\", shell_output(\"#{bin}/bastion 2>&1\", 1)\n62\t  end\n63\tend\n64\tEOF\n65\t\n66\tcat > \"$tap/Casks/bastion.rb\" <<EOF\n67\tcask \"bastion\" do\n68\t  version \"$v\"\n69\t  sha256 \"$(sum \"bastion-desktop-$v-aarch64-apple-darwin.zip\")\"\n70\t\n71\t  url \"$url/bastion-desktop-#{version}-aarch64-apple-darwin.zip\"\n72\t  name \"Bastion\"\n73\t  desc \"Watch and steer the Bastion software factory\"\n74\t  homepage \"https://tacoda.github.io/bastion/\"\n75\t\n76\t  depends_on arch: :arm64\n77\t  depends_on macos: :ventura\n78\t  # The CLI and the server come from the formula, so they are on the path once.\n79\t  depends_on formula: \"tacoda/bastion/bastion\"\n80\t\n81\t  app \"Bastion.app\"\n82\t  binary \"#{appdir}/Bastion.app/Contents/MacOS/bastion-desktop\"\n83\t\n84\t  # Not signed with a Developer ID yet, and Homebrew dropped --no-quarantine:\n85\t  # the download is pinned to its signed sum, so the quarantine mark goes.\n86\t  postflight do\n87\t    system_command \"/usr/bin/xattr\",\n88\t                   args: [\"-dr\", \"com.apple.quarantine\", \"#{appdir}/Bastion.app\"]\n89\t  end\n90\tend\n91\tEOF\n92\t\n93\techo \"wrote $tap/Formula/bastion.rb and $tap/Casks/bastion.rb for $v\"\n94\t\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
{"parentUuid":"323aedef-623f-498a-ba1a-ee48e8b45ae3","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"invoked_skills","skills":[{"name":"voice-check","path":"userSettings:voice-check","content":"Base directory for this skill: /Users/tacoda/.claude/skills/voice-check\n\n# Voice check\n\n**`~/.claude/VOICE.md` is the standard and the only source of truth. Read it\nfirst, every time. Do not review from memory of it, and do not restate its rules\nhere.** This file only covers how to run the check.\n\nThis is the authoritative stage of the VOICE.md section 15 chain, and the only\none that rewrites nothing. `humanizer` handles tone and `slopbeth` handles slop,\nbut both rewrite, so both can hand back what this skill already rejected. That\nis why it runs first to set the constraints and again at the end as the gate.\n\nIt also catches what neither of them can: Ian's own formula, and drift between\npieces.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read `~/.claude/VOICE.md`.\n2. Run the checker:\n   ```bash\n   python3 ~/.claude/skills/voice-check/voicecheck.py PATH \\\n     --corpus ~/tacoda/tacoda.github.io\n   ```\n   `PATH` takes a file or a directory. HTML is stripped to prose; code blocks,\n   styles and scripts are excluded. `--corpus` enables recycled-phrase detection\n   against the published body of work. Exits non-zero when there are findings.\n3. Add `--show-flow 2` to print the paragraphs where sentences stop connecting.\n   That is the fastest way to fix what reads as cold.\n4. Run `humanizer` for tone, then `slopbeth` for slop, then this checker again\n   as the gate. Both rewriters run under the findings from step 2 as hard\n   constraints: neither may reintroduce a banned phrase, an em dash, or a tic\n   already over budget, and neither may add a claim, number, or first-person\n   experience the draft does not already contain. A finding that reappears at\n   the gate gets fixed in the rewriter that put it back.\n5. **Read the prose.** The script reports signals, not judgment. It cannot see a\n   section that answers no reader question, an aphorism doing the work a\n   mechanism should do, or a missing concession.\n6. Report or fix, per what was asked, quoting the exact span for each finding.\n\n## Reading the output\n\nFindings marked `[advisory]` are the deliberate-structure cases in VOICE.md §5:\nparallel rubric labels, non-goals lists, data triads. They usually mean leave it\nalone. Everything else is a real finding.\n\nTwo metrics are easy to misread:\n\n- `reader address` counts \"you\"/\"your\" **and** imperatives, so a page of\n  instructions scores correctly without contorting into second person.\n- `flow` is measured only inside paragraphs of three or more sentences. A\n  reference page made of tables and captions is not scored on it.\n- `over length` counts body prose only, so a page sitting near the 4,500-word cap\n  is longer than that in wall-clock time once its code blocks and tables are\n  read. VOICE.md §16 says what to cut and in what order.\n\n## Rules\n\n- **Preserve every fact.** Numbers, counts, dates, repo names, stated\n  uncertainty, and non-goals survive any rewrite.\n- **Never invent** first-person experience to hit the \"I\" floor. That floor is a\n  request for a fact only Ian has. Ask him for it.\n- **Do not trade one formula for another.** Replacing negation-definitions with\n  clipped aphorisms is not a fix, and splitting a joined clause into two flat\n  sentences to kill an em dash makes the prose worse, not better.\n- **Over-editing strong prose is a failure.** Targets are ranges, not gates. A\n  deliberate violation that serves the reader is fine; say why in the report.\n\n\nARGUMENTS: /Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion-gh-pages/index.html"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"479af3fc-5fa0-45b2-afc5-8659eb3219c2","timestamp":"2026-11-11T04:26:12.703Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nThe following skills were invoked EARLIER in this session (before the conversation was compacted), not on the current turn. They are shown here for context only so you remain aware of their guidelines.\n\nIMPORTANT: Do NOT re-execute these skills or perform their one-time setup actions (e.g., scheduling, creating files) again. Any request or argument text embedded in the skill bodies below — for example under a \"## User Request\" or \"## Input\" heading — was captured when that skill was first invoked. It is NOT the user's current message and NOT a new request: do not act on it as if it were live. Only continue to apply ongoing behavioral guidelines from these skills where still relevant.\n\n### Skill: voice-check\nPath: userSettings:voice-check\n\nBase directory for this skill: /Users/tacoda/.claude/skills/voice-check\n\n# Voice check\n\n**`~/.claude/VOICE.md` is the standard and the only source of truth. Read it\nfirst, every time. Do not review from memory of it, and do not restate its rules\nhere.** This file only covers how to run the check.\n\nThis is the authoritative stage of the VOICE.md section 15 chain, and the only\none that rewrites nothing. `humanizer` handles tone and `slopbeth` handles slop,\nbut both rewrite, so both can hand back what this skill already rejected. That\nis why it runs first to set the constraints and again at the end as the gate.\n\nIt also catches what neither of them can: Ian's own formula, and drift between\npieces.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Read `~/.claude/VOICE.md`.\n2. Run the checker:\n   ```bash\n   python3 ~/.claude/skills/voice-check/voicecheck.py PATH \\\n     --corpus ~/tacoda/tacoda.github.io\n   ```\n   `PATH` takes a file or a directory. HTML is stripped to prose; code blocks,\n   styles and scripts are excluded. `--corpus` enables recycled-phrase detection\n   against the published body of work. Exits non-zero when there are findings.\n3. Add `--show-flow 2` to print the paragraphs where sentences stop connecting.\n   That is the fastest way to fix what reads as cold.\n4. Run `humanizer` for tone, then `slopbeth` for slop, then this checker again\n   as the gate. Both rewriters run under the findings from step 2 as hard\n   constraints: neither may reintroduce a banned phrase, an em dash, or a tic\n   already over budget, and neither may add a claim, number, or first-person\n   experience the draft does not already contain. A finding that reappears at\n   the gate gets fixed in the rewriter that put it back.\n5. **Read the prose.** The script reports signals, not judgment. It cannot see a\n   section that answers no reader question, an aphorism doing the work a\n   mechanism should do, or a missing concession.\n6. Report or fix, per what was asked, quoting the exact span for each finding.\n\n## Reading the output\n\nFindings marked `[advisory]` are the deliberate-structure cases in VOICE.md §5:\nparallel rubric labels, non-goals lists, data triads. They usually mean leave it\nalone. Everything else is a real finding.\n\nTwo metrics are easy to misread:\n\n- `reader address` counts \"you\"/\"your\" **and** imperatives, so a page of\n  instructions scores correctly without contorting into second person.\n- `flow` is measured only inside paragraphs of three or more sentences. A\n  reference page made of tables and captions is not scored on it.\n- `over length` counts body prose only, so a page sitting near the 4,500-word cap\n  is longer than that in wall-clock time once its code blocks and tables are\n  read. VOICE.md §16 says what to cut and in what order.\n\n## Rules\n\n- **Preserve every fact.** Numbers, counts, dates, repo names, stated\n  uncertainty, and non-goals survive any rewrite.\n- **Never invent** first-person experience to hit the \"I\" floor. That floor is a\n  request for a fact only Ian has. Ask him for it.\n- **Do not trade one formula for another.** Replacing negation-definitions with\n  clipped aphorisms is not a fix, and splitting a joined clause into two flat\n  sentences to kill an em dash makes the prose worse, not better.\n- **Over-editing strong prose is a failure.** Targets are ranges, not g