X_SURFACE_ID\" ] && [ \"$CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOKS_DISABLED\" != \"1\" ] && [ -n \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then if [ -n \"${CMUX_SOCKET_PATH:-}\" ]; then CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" --socket \"$CMUX_SOCKET_PATH\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '\\''{}'\\''; }; else CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '\\''{}'\\''; }; fi; else cat >/dev/null; echo '\\''{}'\\''; fi' pre-tool-use 3>&1 1>&2; else cmux_cli=\"${CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOK_CMUX_BIN:-${CMUX_BUNDLED_CLI_PATH:-}}\"; if [ -z \"$cmux_cli\" ] || [ ! -x \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then cmux_cli=\"$(command -v cmux 2>/dev/null || true)\"; fi; agent_pid=\"${CMUX_CLAUDE_PID:-${PPID:-}}\"; if [ -n \"$CMUX_SURFACE_ID\" ] && [ \"$CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOKS_DISABLED\" != \"1\" ] && [ -n \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then if [ -n \"${CMUX_SOCKET_PATH:-}\" ]; then CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" --socket \"$CMUX_SOCKET_PATH\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '{}'; }; else CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '{}'; }; fi; else cat >/dev/null; echo '{}'; fi; fi","durationMs":57},"type":"attachment","uuid":"57dbfbb7-53fe-4cd8-97fb-dfbc6c9b037c","timestamp":"2026-11-11T04:32:24.651Z","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
{"parentUuid":"3c1e28ff-27a9-45b2-9081-587a25c3526e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"305cbff3-a896-4b01-84bb-b6b3038a90f7","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RUJodaYcHCTaQw21jybC1D","type":"tool_result","content":"273:- **F11, the browser agent.** Browser tools for turns, with screenshots and\n303:| F | F1 to F10 done | F1 (2026-11-10): a turn whose provider has no key fails at its start, naming the provider. Retry puts a failed job back in the queue at its step, on its kept worktree, which also revives a job whose VM kept dying; at a stuck step it runs the step again. Extend gives a running turn its full cap again from now, counted from `updated_at`, and a cap timer that fires after an extend waits out the rest. Approving a stuck step reads Ship anyway, and the audit notes the waiver with the first line of what failed. Retry, extend and ship anyway are audit decisions with the person's name, buttons on the job page and `bastion retry` and `bastion extend`. An engine call that failed before the engine got it (`iii is not connected`, a websocket error) is tried again after 0.5, 1, 2 and 4 s; a timed-out call is not, as it may have run, and a sandbox command's own failure is not either. After 10 minutes a running turn shows \"still working\". Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: a command step that exits 3 got stuck, retry ran it again (two results), and approve noted \"shipped anyway by Ian Johnson, waiving: The command failed (exit 3):\"; an extend mid-turn moved the cap's start and was noted; a plan turn on keyless OpenAI failed the job, and with DeepSeek back a retry queued it, gave it a VM, and its plan reached needs_you. The first extend arrived a second after its turn ended and correctly changed nothing. The 10-minute mark has a test, not a live run: the turns took about 10 s. F2 (2026-11-10): a workspace can be edited (its name and path stay), and names a job limit, a revision budget (the steps' send-backs in all, past which the job waits on a person), a branch prefix, setup and cleanup commands, and an environment of `K=V` pairs. Setup runs in a VM of its own, the only one with the network, on the fork point, so it never runs code the agent wrote; what it made reaches the job's offline VM as a tarball streamed through a channel, and stays out of the agent's commits by `.git/info/exclude`. Cleanup runs in the job's VM as it stops, and a failure is logged. The onboarding turn (`POST /workspaces/draft`) belongs to no job: an agent reads the repository in an offline VM, under the gate as a read-only step, and drafts checks, setup, cleanup and environment into the add form; nothing is saved until a person adds the workspace. Live: setup reached PyPI and the job's VM had its files, a clean status, the environment and no network; a 3 MiB upload round-tripped; an edit saved and the store refused a limit of 0. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: an onboarding draft on `demo-local` took 24 s and named pytest, flake8 and a pip setup (its commands then named `/work/repo`, so the brief now says they run from the repository's root); an edit saved every setting and refused a bad prefix and a bare `CI`; a job limit of 2 held a job queued behind three waiting jobs, and raising it admitted the job (an edit now runs the admission pump); setup installed pytest into `.deps` with the network, and the agent's offline VM ran it, 7 passed; the job landed on `main`. The run found two land bugs, both fixed: a repository with no `origin` failed under the GitHub forge (it now lands here), and a VM revived after a restart had no new commits, so git refused the empty bundle (the worktree now lands as it is). F3 (2026-11-10): a workspace names quality gates, one `name: command` per line with `{files}` for the change's added and modified files, and an autofix; before a land or a ship each gate runs in the job's VM, a failure gets the autofix once (committed), and a gate that still fails stops the push with what it printed. A build's diff is checked by four `review::diff` rules: deleted tests, secrets (enforced), assertions lost net of those added, and added skip marks. A rule's words bind what a write writes as well as its arguments. Before a push the pull request's title and body pass a publishing gate: no line crediting an AI as author, and no key, token or private key, each named by kind and never by value; the body no longer says Bastion opened it. A prove turn's yes with no quoted output counts as partial. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: a `todo: ! grep -n TODO {files}` gate failed on the agent's `# TODO` line, the autofix `sed -i /TODO/d {files}` removed it and committed, the gate passed, and the job landed both commits. Not run live: the publishing gate (it needs a GitHub workspace), the proof downgrade (the full pipeline), and the new diff and content rules; each has tests. F4 (2026-11-10): a repository that commits a `verify.yaml` at the job's fork point has each change judged by `gantry-verify` (`@jeger-ai/opengantry-verify` 4.0.2) before a land or a ship, and a refusal stops the push with what it printed. The packaged `opengantry` worker could not do this: it runs sandboxed and cannot see a host repository, and run on the host its `gantry::verify` would run the agent's code outside the VM. So the verify runs in a VM of its own from the `node` image with no network: the job's repository goes in as a tarball, and the package as one installed on the host once with no install scripts. The contract is read from the base, so a change cannot weaken it. The packaged worker and the governed listener on 49161 stay in the stack file, unused. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: on `demo-local` with a contract that runs pytest and forbids `print(`, a change adding `shout(x)` with `print(x)` passed its tests and was refused at `calc.py:30`; the branch stayed as it was. The run found that a turn the harness lost when the stack restarted waited forever; resume now starts such a turn again, which the same run then did. F5 (2026-11-10): one Costs page holds both the costs and the sessions: `GET /costs` tallies every metered turn's cost, turns, calls and failed calls in all, by workspace, by step and by UTC day, and lists the newest 200 turns. A turn opens step by step (`GET /sessions/{id}`): what the agent was asked, said and thought, each call as the gate read it with the decision the gate wrote, and each result, clipped. The Audit Log tallies its entries by kind, the gate's by decision. Exit (2026-11-10), live: `/costs` gave $0.3745 over 84 sessions across three workspaces and four days; the last build of `jfbc0b38c` read as 39 steps with a verdict on each of its 13 calls. F6 (2026-11-10): a learn turn replies with JSON proposals, each a constraint or a workspace gate with why and the evidence quoted from the job; a reply with no evidence is sent back, and the JSON is read wherever it sits after the model's reasoning. The Learning page lists each job's newest readable reply, open proposals first; accepting a constraint adds it as told, accepting a gate adds it to its workspace, and each decision is an audit entry. A land keeps its commits as an artifact; a person reports an escape by its commit, the server traces it to the landed job whose commits hold it and keeps it in the audit, and the Verification page lists each landed job with the decisions that let it through, its commits, its escapes and each workspace's escape rate. An eval's trial that reaches review runs its contract (the workspace's gates without autofix, then `verify.yaml`) before its VM stops, and the eval shows whether it held beside the judge's grade. A local land now goes on to a step after it, such as learn. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: on a workflow of plan, build, land and learn, a job landed three commits and its learn turn found that the autofix had deleted the requested `# TODO: tidy` line and the test assertion that checked it, with pytest still green; it proposed two constraints and a gate, each with evidence. One was declined and the gate accepted into `demo-local` (then removed by hand). An escape on `a7c2732` traced to `j6fd07e99`; a commit no job landed and a 3-digit id were refused. An eval graded both arms 9 and 10 with both contracts held. The run found four bugs, all fixed: a local land ended the job before learn; the reply's JSON came after the reasoning; the contract ran beside the teardown and lost its VM; and the judge ran on the built-in OpenAI default, so no eval run had ever been graded. Accepted constraints cannot be removed yet, and a pull request merged by squash or rebase lands commits an escape cannot trace (F7 fixed this: a merge keeps its merge commit). F7 (2026-11-10): a pull request's body carries the plan, the briefing, the UAT script, the proof and each waiver, and is said again through the publishing gate before the merge, since the proof and the briefing come after it opens. When its checks pass, the preview a host deployed for its branch (the branch's newest GitHub deployment) is kept on the job. UAT, an old checkpoint, is a read-only turn after comprehend that writes a numbered script; it waits whatever its dial, a dark UAT dial is refused, and only the job's requester may approve it. Deploy finds the merge commit and polls its newest deployment: success passes with its address, failure waits on a person, and a commit nothing deploys within two minutes passes with a note. A revert on a workspace's branch of a commit a job landed, its merge commit included, is kept once as that job's escape, swept after each land and merge and before the Verification page reads. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek against `ianparo/bastion-demo` with GitHub Actions: a job on plan, build, ship, CI, prove, comprehend, UAT, merge and deploy opened PR #1, CI passed, the UAT turn wrote a numbered script the requester signed off, the merge said the body again with Plan, Briefing, UAT and Proof and merged as `285b19b`, and deploy passed with \"Nothing deployed 285b19b\". A revert pushed to `main` was kept as the job's escape on the next read, and once only on the one after. The run found that GitHub's Revert button reverts the merge commit, which no land kept; the merge now keeps it. Not run live: a preview and a deployment that succeeds or fails (the demo repository deploys nothing); each has tests. The UAT reply opens with the model's reasoning before its script. F8 (2026-11-10): a request carries a kind (feature, bug, chore or docs), acceptance criteria, links and screenshots; the server writes them into the request's text under their headings and refuses an unknown kind, a link that is not a web address, or a screenshot name that is not a plain image file. Given criteria become the job's. Screenshots, up to 10 MiB in all, are kept on the host under the job asked for, shared by its parts, and put in each job's VM at /work/request. A request may name the model all its turns run on, as provider/model. Scope's parts are kept as its result and split only on approval, a dark scope's at once; while scope waits a person can set them again, and one part or none keeps one job. Intake can be paused in the settings: requests queue and nothing new starts. Every workflow can now be changed, the built-ins too: a changed one is kept under its id and takes its place, and deleting it resets it; the workflows page starts a new one from a name. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: with intake paused two requests stayed queued, and opening it admitted both; one named openai/gpt-5-mini and failed at its first turn with \"openai has no key\", and the other's request read Kind, Acceptance criteria, Links and Screenshots, and its plan found the 29-byte PNG in /work/request; a malformed model and an unknown kind were refused. A scope on a scope, plan, build and land workflow proposed two parts and waited; set again and approved, the job took the first edited part and a second job queued after it with the second. Short was saved as \"Short (changed)\", listed in its place, and reset to the original; a second reset was refused. The CLI does not take the new request fields yet, and whether a model can look at a screenshot is up to the model and its tools. F9 (2026-11-10): a job that comes to wait on a person, fails or lands is a notice: the factory's on_change carries a Change, so beside each job's change it says one line about the job and how many jobs wait. The server publishes notices on its event stream and posts each to a chat webhook as {\"text\": ...} (Slack, Mattermost), kept in a file only the server's user reads and shown to clients as its host and last four characters; Settings sets it, forgets it and sends a test. bastion-tray is a program of its own on tao and tray-icon, because a tray needs the native event loop iced keeps to itself: it reaches the local server through bastion-client, shows each notice as the system's notification, and its menu says how many jobs wait and run, lists the waiting jobs to approve, pauses or opens intake and opens the window; on macOS the waiting count shows beside a white castle. One runs at a time by a lock, the window starts it with its output in tray.log, and it outlives the window. The window shows the waiting count in its title and on Jobs. The app has an icon: the gh-pages castle with its gate lit (assets/icon). Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: a job's plan came to wait, the system notification showed it, the menu bar read the castle and 2, matching the server's two waiting jobs, and the plan was approved and went on to build. The window started the tray once the tray's output was logged; the first start left nothing to say why it stopped. Not run live: the chat webhook (no webhook was set; a test posts a notice to a local listener). The count is in the title and the tray, not a Dock badge, and Open Bastion starts a second window if one is open. F10 (2026-11-10): an installed Bastion keeps its stack in share/bastion beside its binaries (following the link it was run by) and its data in the user's data folder. scripts/package.sh builds a platform's server-and-CLI tarball with its stack (the compose file running Bastion's own worker from its binary), and its desktop: Bastion.app, signed ad hoc until a Developer ID signs it, or a Linux tarball with a desktop entry. A v* tag runs the release workflow on macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64 and arm64, signs SHA256SUMS with the release minisign key and publishes the release. install.sh, served from the site, installs the server and CLI, or with --desktop the app or the window and the tray, checks each file's sum and the sums' signature when minisign is there, and with --service runs the server as a systemd user service on a Linux VM or VPS. bastion update installs the newest release once its signature, built in, and its sum check. scripts/brew.sh writes the tacoda/homebrew-bastion formula and cask from a release's sums. Exit (2026-11-10): v0.1.0 released from all three builds; its signature verified against scripts/minisign.pub; install.sh into a scratch prefix installed the server and the CLI, which ran, and bastion update read v0.1.0 as the newest; --desktop installed Bastion.app, whose ad hoc signature verified, with the CLI linked into it; the tap took the formula and, once fixed (a cask cannot conflict with a formula, and depends_on macos takes :ventura), the cask; the site went out with the install commands and served install.sh. The macOS 14 runner had been retired, so releases run on macos-latest. Not run: --service on a VM, an update across two releases, a Linux install, and the Homebrew installs on this machine. |","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"627f08be-bc72-472e-836e-9c5acfcc1574","timestamp":"2026-11-11T04:32:26.269Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"273:- **F11, the browser agent.** Browser tools for turns, with screenshots and\n303:| F | F1 to F10 done | F1 (2026-11-10): a turn whose provider has no key fails at its start, naming the provider. Retry puts a failed job back in the queue at its step, on its kept worktree, which also revives a job whose VM kept dying; at a stuck step it runs the step again. Extend gives a running turn its full cap again from now, counted from `updated_at`, and a cap timer that fires after an extend waits out the rest. Approving a stuck step reads Ship anyway, and the audit notes the waiver with the first line of what failed. Retry, extend and ship anyway are audit decisions with the person's name, buttons on the job page and `bastion retry` and `bastion extend`. An engine call that failed before the engine got it (`iii is not connected`, a websocket error) is tried again after 0.5, 1, 2 and 4 s; a timed-out call is not, as it may have run, and a sandbox command's own failure is not either. After 10 minutes a running turn shows \"still working\". Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: a command step that exits 3 got stuck, retry ran it again (two results), and approve noted \"shipped anyway by Ian Johnson, waiving: The command failed (exit 3):\"; an extend mid-turn moved the cap's start and was noted; a plan turn on keyless OpenAI failed the job, and with DeepSeek back a retry queued it, gave it a VM, and its plan reached needs_you. The first extend arrived a second after its turn ended and correctly changed nothing. The 10-minute mark has a test, not a live run: the turns took about 10 s. F2 (2026-11-10): a workspace can be edited (its name and path stay), and names a job limit, a revision budget (the steps' send-backs in all, past which the job waits on a person), a branch prefix, setup and cleanup commands, and an environment of `K=V` pairs. Setup runs in a VM of its own, the only one with the network, on the fork point, so it never runs code the agent wrote; what it made reaches the job's offline VM as a tarball streamed through a channel, and stays out of the agent's commits by `.git/info/exclude`. Cleanup runs in the job's VM as it stops, and a failure is logged. The onboarding turn (`POST /workspaces/draft`) belongs to no job: an agent reads the repository in an offline VM, under the gate as a read-only step, and drafts checks, setup, cleanup and environment into the add form; nothing is saved until a person adds the workspace. Live: setup reached PyPI and the job's VM had its files, a clean status, the environment and no network; a 3 MiB upload round-tripped; an edit saved and the store refused a limit of 0. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: an onboarding draft on `demo-local` took 24 s and named pytest, flake8 and a pip setup (its commands then named `/work/repo`, so the brief now says they run from the repository's root); an edit saved every setting and refused a bad prefix and a bare `CI`; a job limit of 2 held a job queued behind three waiting jobs, and raising it admitted the job (an edit now runs the admission pump); setup installed pytest into `.deps` with the network, and the agent's offline VM ran it, 7 passed; the job landed on `main`. The run found two land bugs, both fixed: a repository with no `origin` failed under the GitHub forge (it now lands here), and a VM revived after a restart had no new commits, so git refused the empty bundle (the worktree now lands as it is). F3 (2026-11-10): a workspace names quality gates, one `name: command` per line with `{files}` for the change's added and modified files, and an autofix; before a land or a ship each gate runs in the job's VM, a failure gets the autofix once (committed), and a gate that still fails stops the push with what it printed. A build's diff is checked by four `review::diff` rules: deleted tests, secrets (enforced), assertions lost net of those added, and added skip marks. A rule's words bind what a write writes as well as its arguments. Before a push the pull request's title and body pass a publishing gate: no line crediting an AI as author, and no key, token or private key, each named by kind and never by value; the body no longer says Bastion opened it. A prove turn's yes with no quoted output counts as partial. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: a `todo: ! grep -n TODO {files}` gate failed on the agent's `# TODO` line, the autofix `sed -i /TODO/d {files}` removed it and committed, the gate passed, and the job landed both commits. Not run live: the publishing gate (it needs a GitHub workspace), the proof downgrade (the full pipeline), and the new diff and content rules; each has tests. F4 (2026-11-10): a repository that commits a `verify.yaml` at the job's fork point has each change judged by `gantry-verify` (`@jeger-ai/opengantry-verify` 4.0.2) before a land or a ship, and a refusal stops the push with what it printed. The packaged `opengantry` worker could not do this: it runs sandboxed and cannot see a host repository, and run on the host its `gantry::verify` would run the agent's code outside the VM. So the verify runs in a VM of its own from the `node` image with no network: the job's repository goes in as a tarball, and the package as one installed on the host once with no install scripts. The contract is read from the base, so a change cannot weaken it. The packaged worker and the governed listener on 49161 stay in the stack file, unused. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: on `demo-local` with a contract that runs pytest and forbids `print(`, a change adding `shout(x)` with `print(x)` passed its tests and was refused at `calc.py:30`; the branch stayed as it was. The run found that a turn the harness lost when the stack restarted waited forever; resume now starts such a turn again, which the same run then did. F5 (2026-11-10): one Costs page holds both the costs and the sessions: `GET /costs` tallies every metered turn's cost, turns, calls and failed calls in all, by workspace, by step and by UTC day, and lists the newest 200 turns. A turn opens step by step (`GET /sessions/{id}`): what the agent was asked, said and thought, each call as the gate read it with the decision the gate wrote, and each result, clipped. The Audit Log tallies its entries by kind, the gate's by decision. Exit (2026-11-10), live: `/costs` gave $0.3745 over 84 sessions across three workspaces and four days; the last build of `jfbc0b38c` read as 39 steps with a verdict on each of its 13 calls. F6 (2026-11-10): a learn turn replies with JSON proposals, each a constraint or a workspace gate with why and the evidence quoted from the job; a reply with no evidence is sent back, and the JSON is read wherever it sits after the model's reasoning. The Learning page lists each job's newest readable reply, open proposals first; accepting a constraint adds it as told, accepting a gate adds it to its workspace, and each decision is an audit entry. A land keeps its commits as an artifact; a person reports an escape by its commit, the server traces it to the landed job whose commits hold it and keeps it in the audit, and the Verification page lists each landed job with the decisions that let it through, its commits, its escapes and each workspace's escape rate. An eval's trial that reaches review runs its contract (the workspace's gates without autofix, then `verify.yaml`) before its VM stops, and the eval shows whether it held beside the judge's grade. A local land now goes on to a step after it, such as learn. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: on a workflow of plan, build, land and learn, a job landed three commits and its learn turn found that the autofix had deleted the requested `# TODO: tidy` line and the test assertion that checked it, with pytest still green; it proposed two constraints and a gate, each with evidence. One was declined and the gate accepted into `demo-local` (then removed by hand). An escape on `a7c2732` traced to `j6fd07e99`; a commit no job landed and a 3-digit id were refused. An eval graded both arms 9 and 10 with both contracts held. The run found four bugs, all fixed: a local land ended the job before learn; the reply's JSON came after the reasoning; the contract ran beside the teardown and lost its VM; and the judge ran on the built-in OpenAI default, so no eval run had ever been graded. Accepted constraints cannot be removed yet, and a pull request merged by squash or rebase lands commits an escape cannot trace (F7 fixed this: a merge keeps its merge commit). F7 (2026-11-10): a pull request's body carries the plan, the briefing, the UAT script, the proof and each waiver, and is said again through the publishing gate before the merge, since the proof and the briefing come after it opens. When its checks pass, the preview a host deployed for its branch (the branch's newest GitHub deployment) is kept on the job. UAT, an old checkpoint, is a read-only turn after comprehend that writes a numbered script; it waits whatever its dial, a dark UAT dial is refused, and only the job's requester may approve it. Deploy finds the merge commit and polls its newest deployment: success passes with its address, failure waits on a person, and a commit nothing deploys within two minutes passes with a note. A revert on a workspace's branch of a commit a job landed, its merge commit included, is kept once as that job's escape, swept after each land and merge and before the Verification page reads. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek against `ianparo/bastion-demo` with GitHub Actions: a job on plan, build, ship, CI, prove, comprehend, UAT, merge and deploy opened PR #1, CI passed, the UAT turn wrote a numbered script the requester signed off, the merge said the body again with Plan, Briefing, UAT and Proof and merged as `285b19b`, and deploy passed with \"Nothing deployed 285b19b\". A revert pushed to `main` was kept as the job's escape on the next read, and once only on the one after. The run found that GitHub's Revert button reverts the merge commit, which no land kept; the merge now keeps it. Not run live: a preview and a deployment that succeeds or fails (the demo repository deploys nothing); each has tests. The UAT reply opens with the model's reasoning before its script. F8 (2026-11-10): a request carries a kind (feature, bug, chore or docs), acceptance criteria, links and screenshots; the server writes them into the request's text under their headings and refuses an unknown kind, a link that is not a web address, or a screenshot name that is not a plain image file. Given criteria become the job's. Screenshots, up to 10 MiB in all, are kept on the host under the job asked for, shared by its parts, and put in each job's VM at /work/request. A request may name the model all its turns run on, as provider/model. Scope's parts are kept as its result and split only on approval, a dark scope's at once; while scope waits a person can set them again, and one part or none keeps one job. Intake can be paused in the settings: requests queue and nothing new starts. Every workflow can now be changed, the built-ins too: a changed one is kept under its id and takes its place, and deleting it resets it; the workflows page starts a new one from a name. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: with intake paused two requests stayed queued, and opening it admitted both; one named openai/gpt-5-mini and failed at its first turn with \"openai has no key\", and the other's request read Kind, Acceptance criteria, Links and Screenshots, and its plan found the 29-byte PNG in /work/request; a malformed model and an unknown kind were refused. A scope on a scope, plan, build and land workflow proposed two parts and waited; set again and approved, the job took the first edited part and a second job queued after it with the second. Short was saved as \"Short (changed)\", listed in its place, and reset to the original; a second reset was refused. The CLI does not take the new request fields yet, and whether a model can look at a screenshot is up to the model and its tools. F9 (2026-11-10): a job that comes to wait on a person, fails or lands is a notice: the factory's on_change carries a Change, so beside each job's change it 