ce, and reset to the original; a second reset was refused. The CLI does not take the new request fields yet, and whether a model can look at a screenshot is up to the model and its tools. F9 (2026-11-10): a job that comes to wait on a person, fails or lands is a notice: the factory's on_change carries a Change, so beside each job's change it says one line about the job and how many jobs wait. The server publishes notices on its event stream and posts each to a chat webhook as {\"text\": ...} (Slack, Mattermost), kept in a file only the server's user reads and shown to clients as its host and last four characters; Settings sets it, forgets it and sends a test. bastion-tray is a program of its own on tao and tray-icon, because a tray needs the native event loop iced keeps to itself: it reaches the local server through bastion-client, shows each notice as the system's notification, and its menu says how many jobs wait and run, lists the waiting jobs to approve, pauses or opens intake and opens the window; on macOS the waiting count shows beside a white castle. One runs at a time by a lock, the window starts it with its output in tray.log, and it outlives the window. The window shows the waiting count in its title and on Jobs. The app has an icon: the gh-pages castle with its gate lit (assets/icon). Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: a job's plan came to wait, the system notification showed it, the menu bar read the castle and 2, matching the server's two waiting jobs, and the plan was approved and went on to build. The window started the tray once the tray's output was logged; the first start left nothing to say why it stopped. Not run live: the chat webhook (no webhook was set; a test posts a notice to a local listener). The count is in the title and the tray, not a Dock badge, and Open Bastion starts a second window if one is open. F10 (2026-11-10): an installed Bastion keeps its stack in share/bastion beside its binaries (following the link it was run by) and its data in the user's data folder. scripts/package.sh builds a platform's server-and-CLI tarball with its stack (the compose file running Bastion's own worker from its binary), and its desktop: Bastion.app, signed ad hoc until a Developer ID signs it, or a Linux tarball with a desktop entry. A v* tag runs the release workflow on macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64 and arm64, signs SHA256SUMS with the release minisign key and publishes the release. install.sh, served from the site, installs the server and CLI, or with --desktop the app or the window and the tray, checks each file's sum and the sums' signature when minisign is there, and with --service runs the server as a systemd user service on a Linux VM or VPS. bastion update installs the newest release once its signature, built in, and its sum check. scripts/brew.sh writes the tacoda/homebrew-bastion formula and cask from a release's sums. Exit (2026-11-10): v0.1.0 released from all three builds; its signature verified against scripts/minisign.pub; install.sh into a scratch prefix installed the server and the CLI, which ran, and bastion update read v0.1.0 as the newest; --desktop installed Bastion.app, whose ad hoc signature verified, with the CLI linked into it; the tap took the formula and, once fixed (a cask cannot conflict with a formula, and depends_on macos takes :ventura), the cask; the site went out with the install commands and served install.sh. The macOS 14 runner had been retired, so releases run on macos-latest. Not run: --service on a VM, an update across two releases, a Linux install, and the Homebrew installs on this machine. F11 (2026-11-10): a workspace names its VM image, python, node or browser; browser is Microsoft's Playwright image (v1.65.0, noble) registered with the sandbox worker, with Node, ffmpeg and Chromium, Firefox and WebKit under /ms-playwright but not the playwright npm package. Every VM now boots with loopback up: an offline VM had `lo` down, so nothing could serve on 127.0.0.1, and with no `ip` in the images the factory brings it up with Perl's ioctl, a failure logged and harmless. A browser VM gets PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH, which the image's own environment does not pass, and its turns are told how to take a screenshot offline and that a local server must start in the same command as the browser. After each turn the factory copies each image or recording under /work/artifacts, up to 50 MB each, to the host once, keeps it on the job, and serves it at `GET /jobs/{id}/media/{name}`; the job page lists the kept files to open. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: the browser image took about 9 minutes to pull on first boot; with loopback down a local request timed out, and up it answered. On a `demo-browser` workspace a short-pipeline job added a paragraph, served the site with node and screenshotted it with the headless shell; home.png (1280 by 800, showing the new paragraph) was kept on the job and served by the media route. Not run live: a recording, and a workspace whose setup installs playwright to drive a script; the job page's Kept files section has no live look either. |"]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/PLAN.md"]}},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"8a9292c2-b120-467c-a8cb-6e914f16446f","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","root":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":null,"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[],"visibility_cache":[]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d1b6dddb-4d59-4538-8b77-9e9baf3c8d5a","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
