{"parentUuid":"01d903e8-20bb-4260-821a-32af5df65bbf","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"64ff152e-5b2e-4d1d-9752-6ec3f79e2721","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011D3sZSkF4EV3RngPmCeoqW","type":"tool_result","content":"[entire] Pushing 2 checkpoint ref(s) to origin....... done\nc847db8 F11's recording runs live, on a workspace whose setup installs playwright","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"e4467b3f-89a5-492e-9076-2bd5d3fb1bc7","timestamp":"2026-11-11T04:56:43.068Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"[entire] Pushing 2 checkpoint ref(s) to origin....... done\nc847db8 F11's recording runs live, on a workspace whose setup installs playwright","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false,"bashEditDiff":{"files":[{"filePath":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/PLAN.md","hunks":[{"oldStart":300,"oldLines":4,"newStart":300,"newLines":4,"lines":[" | C | done | C1 retired the old client; C2 removed the protocol and server parts only it used, and the client's profiles; C3 removed the fixed grading model, the dead queue, spikes N2, N12's server half and N14, the people and token type duplicates, every citation of an old plan phase, and the old client's words in server code. C4 removed the stack's `cron`, `storage`, `ide`, `workflow`, `memory` and `eval`, which nothing calls; `harness` calls `session-manager`, `context-manager`, `iii-directory`, `fp`, `judge` and `approval-gate` (from its binary), so they stay; `opengantry` stays for F4. C5 rewrote the README and CLAUDE.md, and the spikes crate went: its findings are in the code, and their record is `docs/engine-findings.md`. Exit, live (2026-11-09): the server and the stack restarted on the trimmed stack file, a short workflow's plan turn reached Approve, and the desktop client listed it; CI's steps pass with no Tauri, Leptos or wasm step. C6, with the owner's yes, deleted the untracked `floyd` data and config, 885 MB of engine telemetry, a database backup, `agents/` and `skills/provider-claude-code`. |"," | R | done | R1 (2026-11-09): the audit writer reads only the chain's tail, keeps each record in `audit-spool.jsonl` until it is written, tries a failed write again with backoff up to 30 s, and keeps the newest entry's sequence and hash in `audit-anchor`, so deleting entries from the end breaks the chain. Live: a stack stop's record waited in the spool while the stack was down and was written, with the start's, once it came back (entries 1347 and 1348), and the chain verified. The queue stays unbounded, as the spool keeps it on disk; a stop's record no longer needs writing before the stop. R2 (2026-11-09): a turn's session is `<job>-<step>-<random>`, and its result applies only when the record still owns that session at that step, checked inside the save (`apply_when`); a turn that ends twice counts once. Resume runs at start and each time the engine link comes back up, re-arms what is left of a turn's cap, starts a running factory step's work again (a land with its pull request up watches CI again), and a queued part whose earlier part failed is abandoned. Live: the server was restarted mid-turn and the job still reached needs_you. R3 (2026-11-09): the factory reads the charter and the brief's charter files with `git show <fork point>:<file>`, so a file the agent changes in its worktree binds nothing; the Govern page still reads the workspace's own checkout. The core gate refuses a `sandbox::fs::write` to `CLAUDE.md` or `AGENTS.md` at any depth, or under `.claude/`, before any constraint. A shell command can still write those files; reading from the base commit is what keeps that from binding. R4 (2026-11-09): allow and refuse claim a held call with `DELETE … RETURNING id`, so two settles at once run it once. The baseline stores the approved test files in the job's `approved` column, and each turn start and resume loads them into the gate. Teardown and the turn cap call `harness::stop`. Stack start and stop share one lock, and so do writes to the router entry (per server process). `apply_when` returns the job as it was before the event moved it, and `decide` credits from that. Not checked live: the live store tests wait forever for an engine link, and the engine on 49160 did not give one. R5 (2026-11-09): every host command stops after 300 s, with no stdin and `GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT=0`. The desktop client's calls get 5 s to connect and 120 s in all; its event stream reads as down after 45 s of silence. The openai-compat worker ends a request silent for 120 s and reads its kept hosts again at start until state answers. Each TLS handshake runs in its own task. R6 (2026-11-09): the admission pump and resume read only unfinished jobs and the jobs a queued part waits on. `GET /jobs` and `GET /audit` take `limit` (200 by default, 1000 at most) and `offset`; `/audit` still reads the whole chain to verify it. A row that does not parse is logged and left out of the browse lists; the audit chain and the gate's rules stay strict. R7 (2026-11-09): a missing job answers 404; a failure while the engine link is down answers 503. The owner token is compared in constant time. The token file and, where there is no Keychain, the audit key file are made with mode 0600. The client refuses `http://` beyond loopback; a server added before this keeps its URL. The stack starts `iii compose` with only the basic environment and `III_*`, so worker VMs get no host secrets; a provider whose key came from the environment needs its key set in Settings. R exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: two Allows on one held call ran it once (one `act`, the second refused as settled); the server restarted mid-Build and the turn resumed to needs_you; the server stopped took its stack down, and on start the stack came back, a mid-plan job resumed to needs_you and a waiting job stayed put; the audit chain verified with no gap throughout. The live store tests found that the engine refuses a second worker named `bastion-app`; the name now carries the process id. |"," | I | done | I1 (2026-11-10): `crates/bastion-client` holds the server list, the local start, the calls and the event stream; the desktop keeps only its iced subscription. I2: every route is a typed request in `bastion_protocol::api` with its answer type; the server's tests check each against the router and each body against its handler; the command table is gone. I3: tokens stay in the Keychain on macOS and in 0600 files elsewhere; a server without a readable token is listed and its calls say why; clients trust the OS trust store, so a private CA works. I4: `bastion` lists servers and jobs, submits, approves, sends back, stops and follows. Exit (2026-11-10), live: a TLS server on the LAN with a test-CA certificate; the CLI sent a job back and the desktop showed the new turn; the desktop stopped it and `bastion follow` printed the change. The run found two bugs, both fixed: clients and a hand-started server used different data folders (so different tokens), and a turn started on a VM that went while its job waited. |","-| F | F1 to F11 done | F1 (2026-11-10): a turn whose provider has no key fails at its start, naming the provider. Retry puts a failed job back in the queue at its step, on its kept worktree, which also revives a job whose VM kept dying; at a stuck step it runs the step again. Extend gives a running turn its full cap again from now, counted from `updated_at`, and a cap timer that fires after an extend waits out the rest. Approving a stuck step reads Ship anyway, and the audit notes the waiver with the first line of what failed. Retry, extend and ship anyway are audit decisions with the person's name, buttons on the job page and `bastion retry` and `bastion extend`. An engine call that failed before the engine got it (`iii is not connected`, a websocket error) is tried again after 0.5, 1, 2 and 4 s; a timed-out call is not, as it may have run, and a sandbox command's own failure is not either. After 10 minutes a running turn shows \"still working\". Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: a command step that exits 3 got stuck, retry ran it again (two results), and approve noted \"shipped anyway by Ian Johnson, waiving: The command failed (exit 3):\"; an extend mid-turn moved the cap's start and was noted; a plan turn on keyless OpenAI failed the job, and with DeepSeek back a retry queued it, gave it a VM, and its plan reached needs_you. The first extend arrived a second after its turn ended and correctly changed nothing. The 10-minute mark has a test, not a live run: the turns took about 10 s. F2 (2026-11-10): a workspace can be edited (its name and path stay), and names a job limit, a revision budget (the steps' send-backs in all, past which the job waits on a person), a branch prefix, setup and cleanup commands, and an environment of `K=V` pairs. Setup runs in a VM of its own, the only one with the network, on the fork point, so it never runs code the agent wrote; what it made reaches the job's offline VM as a tarball streamed through a channel, and stays out of the agent's commits by `.git/info/exclude`. Cleanup runs in the job's VM as it stops, and a failure is logged. The onboarding turn (`POST /workspaces/draft`) belongs to no job: an agent reads the repository in an offline VM, under the gate as a read-only step, and drafts checks, setup, cleanup and environment into the add form; nothing is saved until a person adds the workspace. Live: setup reached PyPI and the job's VM had its files, a clean status, the environment and no network; a 3 MiB upload round-tripped; an edit saved and the store refused a limit of 0. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: an onboarding draft on `demo-local` took 24 s and named pytest, flake8 and a pip setup (its commands then named `/work/repo`, so the brief now says they run from the repository's root); an edit saved every setting and refused a bad prefix and a bare `CI`; a job limit of 2 held a job queued behind three waiting jobs, and raising it admitted the job (an edit now runs the admission pump); setup installed pytest into `.deps` with the network, and the agent's offline VM ran it, 7 passed; the job landed on `main`. The run found two land bugs, both fixed: a repository with no `origin` failed under the GitHub forge (it now lands here), and a VM revived after a restart had no new commits, so git refused the empty bundle (the worktree now lands as it is). F3 (2026-11-10): a workspace names quality gates, one `name: command` per line with `{files}` for the change's added and modified files, and an autofix; before a land or a ship each gate runs in the job's VM, a failure gets the autofix once (committed), and a gate that still fails stops the push with what it printed. A build's diff is checked by four `review::diff` rules: deleted tests, secrets (enforced), assertions lost net of those added, and added skip marks. A rule's words bind what a write writes as well as its arguments. Before a push the pull request's title and body pass a publishing gate: no line crediting an AI as author, and no key, token or private key, each named by kind and never by value; the body no longer says Bastion opened it. A prove turn's yes with no quoted output counts as partial. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: a `todo: ! grep -n TODO {files}` gate failed on the agent's `# TODO` line, the autofix `sed -i /TODO/d {files}` removed it and committed, the gate passed, and the job landed both commits. Not run live: the publishing gate (it needs a GitHub workspace), the proof downgrade (the full pipeline), and the new diff and content rules; each has tests. F4 (2026-11-10): a repository that commits a `verify.yaml` at the job's fork point has each change judged by `gantry-verify` (`@jeger-ai/opengantry-verify` 4.0.2) before a land or a ship, and a refusal stops the push with what it printed. The packaged `opengantry` worker could not do this: it runs sandboxed and cannot see a host repository, and run on the host its `gantry::verify` would run the agent's code outside the VM. So the verify runs in a VM of its own from the `node` image with no network: the job's repository goes in as a tarball, and the package as one installed on the host once with no install scripts. The contract is read from the base, so a change cannot weaken it. The packaged worker and the governed listener on 49161 stay in the stack file, unused. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: on `demo-local` with a contract that runs pytest and forbids `print(`, a change adding `shout(x)` with `print(x)` passed its tests and was refused at `calc.py:30`; the branch stayed as it was. The run found that a turn the harness lost when the stack restarted waited forever; resume now starts such a turn again, which the same run then did. F5 (2026-11-10): one Costs page holds both the costs and the sessions: `GET /costs` tallies every metered turn's cost, turns, calls and failed calls in all, by workspace, by step and by UTC day, and lists the newest 200 turns. A turn opens step by step (`GET /sessions/{id}`): what the agent was asked, said and thought, each call as the gate read it with the decision the gate wrote, and each result, clipped. The Audit Log tallies its entries by kind, the gate's by decision. Exit (2026-11-10), live: `/costs` gave $0.3745 over 84 sessions across three workspaces and four days; the last build of `jfbc0b38c` read as 39 steps with a verdict on each of its 13 calls. F6 (2026-11-10): a learn turn replies with JSON proposals, each a constraint or a workspace gate with why and the evidence quoted from the job; a reply with no evidence is sent back, and the JSON is read wherever it sits after the model's reasoning. The Learning page lists each job's newest readable reply, open proposals first; accepting a constraint adds it as told, accepting a gate adds it to its workspace, and each decision is an audit entry. A land keeps its commits as an artifact; a person reports an escape by its commit, the server traces it to the landed job whose commits hold it and keeps it in the audit, and the Verification page lists each landed job with the decisions that let it through, its commits, its escapes and each workspace's escape rate. An eval's trial that reaches review runs its contract (the workspace's gates without autofix, then `verify.yaml`) before its VM stops, and the eval shows whether it held beside the judge's grade. A local land now goes on to a step after it, such as learn. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: on a workflow of plan, build, land and learn, a job landed three commits and its learn turn found that the autofix had deleted the requested `# TODO: tidy` line and the test assertion that checked it, with pytest still green; it proposed two constraints and a gate, each with evidence. One was declined and the gate accepted into `demo-local` (then removed by hand). An escape on `a7c2732` traced to `j6fd07e99`; a commit no job landed and a 3-digit id were refused. An eval graded both arms 9 and 10 with both contracts held. The run found four bugs, all fixed: a local land ended the job before learn; the reply's JSON came after the reasoning; the contract ran beside the teardown and lost its VM; and the judge ran on the built-in OpenAI default, so no eval run had ever been graded. Accepted constraints cannot be removed yet, and a pull request merged by squash or rebase lands commits an escape cannot trace (F7 fixed this: a merge keeps its merge commit). F7 (2026-11-10): a pull request's body carries the plan, the briefing, the UAT script, the proof and each waiver, and is said again through the publishing gate before the merge, since the proof and the briefing come after it opens. When its checks pass, the preview a host deployed for its branch (the branch's newest GitHub deployment) is kept on the job. UAT, an old checkpoint, is a read-only turn after comprehend that writes a numbered script; it waits whatever its dial, a dark UAT dial is refused, and only the job's requester may approve it. Deploy finds the merge commit and polls its newest deployment: success passes with its address, failure waits on a person, and a commit nothing deploys within two minutes passes with a note. A revert on a workspace's branch of a commit a job landed, its merge commit included, is kept once as that job's escape, swept after each land and merge and before the Verification page reads. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek against `ianparo/bastion-demo` with GitHub Actions: a job on plan, build, ship, CI, prove, comprehend, UAT, merge and deploy opened PR #1, CI passed, the UAT turn wrote a numbered script the requester signed off, the merge said the body again with Plan, Briefing, UAT and Proof and merged as `285b19b`, and deploy passed with \"Nothing deployed 285b19b\". A revert pushed to `main` was kept as the job's escape on the next read, and once only on the one after. The run found that GitHub's Revert button reverts the merge commit, which no land kept; the merge now keeps it. Not run live: a preview and a deployment that succeeds or fails (the demo repository deploys nothing); each has tests. The UAT reply opens with the model's reasoning before its script. F8 (2026-11-10): a request carries a kind (feature, bug, chore or docs), acceptance criteria, links and screenshots; the server writes them into the request's text under their headings and refuses an unknown kind, a link that is not a web address, or a screenshot name that is not a plain image file. Given criteria become the job's. Screenshots, up to 10 MiB in all, are kept on the host under the job asked for, shared by its parts, and put in each job's VM at /work/request. A request may name the model all its turns run on, as provider/model. Scope's parts are kept as its result and split only on approval, a dark scope's at once; while scope waits a person can set them again, and one part or none keeps one job. Intake can be paused in the settings: requests queue and nothing new starts. Every workflow can now be changed, the built-ins too: a changed one is kept under its id and takes its place, and deleting it resets it; the workflows page starts a new one from a name. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: with intake paused two requests stayed queued, and opening it admitted both; one named openai/gpt-5-mini and failed at its first turn with \"openai has no key\", and the other's request read Kind, Acceptance criteria, Links and Screenshots, and its plan found the 29-byte PNG in /work/request; a malformed model and an unknown kind were refused. A scope on a scope, plan, build and land workflow proposed two parts and waited; set again and approved, the job took the first edited part and a second job queued after it with the second. Short was saved as \"Short (changed)\", listed in its place, and reset to the original; a second reset was refused. The CLI does not take the new request fields yet, and whether a model can look at a screenshot is up to the model and its tools. F9 (2026-11-10): a job that comes to wait on a person, fails or lands is a notice: the factory's on_change carries a Change, so beside each job's change it says one line about the job and how many jobs wait. The server publishes notices on its event stream and posts each to a chat webhook as {\"text\": ...} (Slack, Mattermost), kept in a file only the server's user reads and shown to clients as its host and last four characters; Settings sets it, forgets it and sends a test. bastion-tray is a program of its own on tao and tray-icon, because a tray needs the native event loop iced keeps to itself: it reaches the local server through bastion-client, shows each notice as the system's notification, and its menu says how many jobs wait and run, lists the waiting jobs to approve, pauses or opens intake and opens the window; on macOS the waiting count shows beside a white castle. One runs at a time by a lock, the window starts it with its output in tray.log, and it outlives the window. The window shows the waiting count in its title and on Jobs. The app has an icon: the gh-pages castle with its gate lit (assets/icon). Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: a job's plan came to wait, the system notification showed it, the menu bar read the castle and 2, matching the server's two waiting jobs, and the plan was approved and went on to build. The window started the tray once the tray's output was logged; the first start left nothing to say why it stopped. Not run live: the chat webhook (no webhook was set; a test posts a notice to a local listener). The count is in the title and the tray, not a Dock badge, and Open Bastion starts a second window if one is open. F10 (2026-11-10): an installed Bastion keeps its stack in share/bastion beside its binaries (following the link it was run by) and its data in the user's data folder. scripts/package.sh builds a platform's server-and-CLI tarball with its stack (the compose file running Bastion's own worker from its binary), and its desktop: Bastion.app, signed ad hoc until a Developer ID signs it, or a Linux tarball with a desktop entry. A v* tag runs the release workflow on macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64 and arm64, signs SHA256SUMS with the release minisign key and publishes the release. install.sh, served from the site, installs the server and CLI, or with --desktop the app or the window and the tray, checks each file's sum and the sums' signature when minisign is there, and with --service runs the server as a systemd user service on a Linux VM or VPS. bastion update installs the newest release once its signature, built in, and its sum check. scripts/brew.sh writes the tacoda/homebrew-bastion formula and cask from a release's sums. Exit (2026-11-10): v0.1.0 released from all three builds; its signature verified against scripts/minisign.pub; install.sh into a scratch prefix installed the server and the CLI, which ran, and bastion update read v0.1.0 as the newest; --desktop installed Bastion.app, whose ad hoc signature verified, with the CLI linked into it; the tap took the formula and, once fixed (a cask cannot conflict with a formula, and depends_on macos takes :ventura), the cask; the site went out with the install commands and served install.sh. The macOS 14 runner had been retired, so releases run on macos-latest. Not run: --service on a VM, an update across two releases, a Linux install, and the Homebrew installs on this machine. F11 (2026-11-10): a workspace names its VM image, python, node or browser; browser is Microsoft's Playwright image (v1.65.0, noble) registered with the sandbox worker, with Node, ffmpeg and Chromium, Firefox and WebKit under /ms-playwright but not the playwright npm package. Every VM now boots with loopback up: an offline VM had `lo` down, so nothing could serve on 127.0.0.1, and with no `ip` in the images the factory brings it up with Perl's ioctl, a failure logged and harmless. A browser VM gets PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH, which the image's own environment does not pass, and its turns are told how to take a screenshot offline and that a local server must start in the same command as the browser. After each turn the factory copies each image or recording under /work/artifacts, up to 50 MB each, to the host once, keeps it on the job, and serves it at `GET /jobs/{id}/media/{name}`; the job page lists the kept files to open. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: the browser image took about 9 minutes to pull on first boot; with loopback down a local request timed out, and up it answered. On a `demo-browser` workspace a short-pipeline job added a paragraph, served the site with node and screenshotted it with the headless shell; home.png (1280 by 800, showing the new paragraph) was kept on the job and served by the media route. Not run live: a recording, and a workspace whose setup installs playwright to drive a script; the job page's Kept files section has no live look either. |","+| F | F1 to F11 done | F1 (2026-11-10): a turn whose provider has no key fails at its start