 landed and a 3-digit id were refused. An eval graded both arms 9 and 10 with both contracts held. The run found four bugs, all fixed: a local land ended the job before learn; the reply's JSON came after the reasoning; the contract ran beside the teardown and lost its VM; and the judge ran on the built-in OpenAI default, so no eval run had ever been graded. Accepted constraints cannot be removed yet, and a pull request merged by squash or rebase lands commits an escape cannot trace (F7 fixed this: a merge keeps its merge commit). F7 (2026-11-10): a pull request's body carries the plan, the briefing, the UAT script, the proof and each waiver, and is said again through the publishing gate before the merge, since the proof and the briefing come after it opens. When its checks pass, the preview a host deployed for its branch (the branch's newest GitHub deployment) is kept on the job. UAT, an old checkpoint, is a read-only turn after comprehend that writes a numbered script; it waits whatever its dial, a dark UAT dial is refused, and only the job's requester may approve it. Deploy finds the merge commit and polls its newest deployment: success passes with its address, failure waits on a person, and a commit nothing deploys within two minutes passes with a note. A revert on a workspace's branch of a commit a job landed, its merge commit included, is kept once as that job's escape, swept after each land and merge and before the Verification page reads. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek against `ianparo/bastion-demo` with GitHub Actions: a job on plan, build, ship, CI, prove, comprehend, UAT, merge and deploy opened PR #1, CI passed, the UAT turn wrote a numbered script the requester signed off, the merge said the body again with Plan, Briefing, UAT and Proof and merged as `285b19b`, and deploy passed with \"Nothing deployed 285b19b\". A revert pushed to `main` was kept as the job's escape on the next read, and once only on the one after. The run found that GitHub's Revert button reverts the merge commit, which no land kept; the merge now keeps it. Not run live: a preview and a deployment that succeeds or fails (the demo repository deploys nothing); each has tests. The UAT reply opens with the model's reasoning before its script. F8 (2026-11-10): a request carries a kind (feature, bug, chore or docs), acceptance criteria, links and screenshots; the server writes them into the request's text under their headings and refuses an unknown kind, a link that is not a web address, or a screenshot name that is not a plain image file. Given criteria become the job's. Screenshots, up to 10 MiB in all, are kept on the host under the job asked for, shared by its parts, and put in each job's VM at /work/request. A request may name the model all its turns run on, as provider/model. Scope's parts are kept as its result and split only on approval, a dark scope's at once; while scope waits a person can set them again, and one part or none keeps one job. Intake can be paused in the settings: requests queue and nothing new starts. Every workflow can now be changed, the built-ins too: a changed one is kept under its id and takes its place, and deleting it resets it; the workflows page starts a new one from a name. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: with intake paused two requests stayed queued, and opening it admitted both; one named openai/gpt-5-mini and failed at its first turn with \"openai has no key\", and the other's request read Kind, Acceptance criteria, Links and Screenshots, and its plan found the 29-byte PNG in /work/request; a malformed model and an unknown kind were refused. A scope on a scope, plan, build and land workflow proposed two parts and waited; set again and approved, the job took the first edited part and a second job queued after it with the second. Short was saved as \"Short (changed)\", listed in its place, and reset to the original; a second reset was refused. The CLI does not take the new request fields yet, and whether a model can look at a screenshot is up to the model and its tools. F9 (2026-11-10): a job that comes to wait on a person, fails or lands is a notice: the factory's on_change carries a Change, so beside each job's change it says one line about the job and how many jobs wait. The server publishes notices on its event stream and posts each to a chat webhook as {\"text\": ...} (Slack, Mattermost), kept in a file only the server's user reads and shown to clients as its host and last four characters; Settings sets it, forgets it and sends a test. bastion-tray is a program of its own on tao and tray-icon, because a tray needs the native event loop iced keeps to itself: it reaches the local server through bastion-client, shows each notice as the system's notification, and its menu says how many jobs wait and run, lists the waiting jobs to approve, pauses or opens intake and opens the window; on macOS the waiting count shows beside a white castle. One runs at a time by a lock, the window starts it with its output in tray.log, and it outlives the window. The window shows the waiting count in its title and on Jobs. The app has an icon: the gh-pages castle with its gate lit (assets/icon). Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: a job's plan came to wait, the system notification showed it, the menu bar read the castle and 2, matching the server's two waiting jobs, and the plan was approved and went on to build. The window started the tray once the tray's output was logged; the first start left nothing to say why it stopped. Not run live: the chat webhook (no webhook was set; a test posts a notice to a local listener). The count is in the title and the tray, not a Dock badge, and Open Bastion starts a second window if one is open. F10 (2026-11-10): an installed Bastion keeps its stack in share/bastion beside its binaries (following the link it was run by) and its data in the user's data folder. scripts/package.sh builds a platform's server-and-CLI tarball with its stack (the compose file running Bastion's own worker from its binary), and its desktop: Bastion.app, signed ad hoc until a Developer ID signs it, or a Linux tarball with a desktop entry. A v* tag runs the release workflow on macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64 and arm64, signs SHA256SUMS with the release minisign key and publishes the release. install.sh, served from the site, installs the server and CLI, or with --desktop the app or the window and the tray, checks each file's sum and the sums' signature when minisign is there, and with --service runs the server as a systemd user service on a Linux VM or VPS. bastion update installs the newest release once its signature, built in, and its sum check. scripts/brew.sh writes the tacoda/homebrew-bastion formula and cask from a release's sums. Exit (2026-11-10): v0.1.0 released from all three builds; its signature verified against scripts/minisign.pub; install.sh into a scratch prefix installed the server and the CLI, which ran, and bastion update read v0.1.0 as the newest; --desktop installed Bastion.app, whose ad hoc signature verified, with the CLI linked into it; the tap took the formula and, once fixed (a cask cannot conflict with a formula, and depends_on macos takes :ventura), the cask; the site went out with the install commands and served install.sh. The macOS 14 runner had been retired, so releases run on macos-latest. Not run: --service on a VM, an update across two releases, a Linux install, and the Homebrew installs on this machine. F11 (2026-11-10): a workspace names its VM image, python, node or browser; browser is Microsoft's Playwright image (v1.65.0, noble) registered with the sandbox worker, with Node, ffmpeg and Chromium, Firefox and WebKit under /ms-playwright but not the playwright npm package. Every VM now boots with loopback up: an offline VM had `lo` down, so nothing could serve on 127.0.0.1, and with no `ip` in the images the factory brings it up with Perl's ioctl, a failure logged and harmless. A browser VM gets PLAYWRIGHT_BROWSERS_PATH, which the image's own environment does not pass, and its turns are told how to take a screenshot offline and that a local server must start in the same command as the browser. After each turn the factory copies each image or recording under /work/artifacts, up to 50 MB each, to the host once, keeps it on the job, and serves it at `GET /jobs/{id}/media/{name}`; the job page lists the kept files to open. Exit (2026-11-10), live on DeepSeek: the browser image took about 9 minutes to pull on first boot; with loopback down a local request timed out, and up it answered. On a `demo-browser` workspace a short-pipeline job added a paragraph, served the site with node and screenshotted it with the headless shell; home.png (1280 by 800, showing the new paragraph) was kept on the job and served by the media route. With its setup installing playwright@1.65.0 (in the setup VM, with the network), a second job wrote a Playwright script that served the site in its own process, recorded Chromium clicking a new Back to top link, and kept visit.webm (25 KB) and after.png; both played back right from the media route, and the setup's node_modules stayed out of the diff. The desktop's job page listed both under Kept files, and Open saved after.png to the temporary folder and opened it in Preview. |"]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/PLAN.md"]}},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"62c00bf1-3c14-4692-8831-523553953bd1","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","root":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":null,"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[],"visibility_cache":[]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"de58bfa4-90fd-4690-a372-4b2b9f91431c","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5